docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:3:This historical record explains the original dotted storage keys and the reasoning behind the preferences key.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:5:Status: Superseded by ADR 058: storage namespace
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:9:The first persistent data was small: a chosen grid size and local best move counts. It lived beside other applications on a browser origin, so a generic key such as `settings` would have been easy to collide with. The storage discussion treated key naming as a way to identify ownership and make saved data recognizable during debugging.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:11:The decision at that time was that stored data uses keys of the form `neon-pairs.THING.v1`. The prefix identified the game, the middle segment identified the data family and the suffix identified the stored format generation. The existing preferences key, `neon-pairs.preferences.v1`, follows that decision. Its name was intended to be understandable in browser storage tools without requiring a separate registry.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:15:Combining size and best moves in one preferences value made the initial storage surface easy to inspect. A reload could read one JSON document and either accept its supported values or return a default size with no best records. The document also carried `schemaVersion: 1`, so the reader could check the structure after locating the key.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:17:The duplication between a versioned key and a version field was deliberate. The key selected a family of stored data; the field protected the parser from an unrelated or damaged value placed there. A recognizable name did not establish that the JSON was valid. Preferences store describes the actual parser.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:21:One unqualified key for the whole application would have been shorter. It would also have hidden ownership and made unrelated applications sharing an origin more likely to reuse the name. A separate key for each score would have made individual entries easy to inspect but spread validation and writes over several values.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:27:The storage adapter exposed `getItem` and `setItem`, allowing a fake store to throw during tests. Parsing, access and writing could all fail without preventing the in-memory game from proceeding. Reading a versioned key therefore did not imply that persistence was always available. The browser origin still controlled access, and direct disk opening had browser-dependent storage behavior.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:29:The reader checked the saved side length against the supported catalogue and checked score entries against the move limits. A key name was not used as a substitute for these checks. Similarly, ownership of one key gave no reason to clear other origin data when a value was rejected.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:33:This record explains why a dotted versioned preferences key exists in the current source. It is not the naming authority for additional stored families. The replacement decision is ADR 058; it addresses new names without requiring the current preferences value to be renamed.
docs/adr-011-storage-keys.md:35:That distinction prevents a maintenance error in which a developer reads an old record, treats its original rationale as current policy and spreads the retired naming scheme. It also preserves the useful part of the history: the preferences name was a considered design choice, not an arbitrary leftover. Persistence envelope covers the current value and Corrupt data policy covers rejection behavior.
docs/adr-013-monotonic-clock.md:13:The useful question is how much active time passed between two observations, not what civil time the machine currently reports. A monotonic source is suited to that interval measurement. The browser adapter keeps an `observedAt` value in `ActiveClock`; `elapsedStep` derives the delta and always returns a new observation baseline.
docs/adr-015-replay-cli.md:9:A replay describes one deal with a board code, timed card indices and an end time. It is especially useful for an error that depends on the order of a timeout and an activation. A snapshot answers what the state was; a replay can answer how the stated actions reached a state. Replay format documents the input.
docs/adr-015-replay-cli.md:27:A browser replay UI could have supplied a step button and visual debugging. It would also have added interaction design, import messages and state ownership for a feature primarily used by engineers. Replaying through Playwright alone would have been possible, but every logical investigation would then require a browser and generated input events.
docs/adr-017-colocated-tests.md:33:A central unit-test directory was considered simpler to scan by test type, but it detached examples from the functions under review. The current organization favors the engineer opening one behavior directory to understand both implementation and evidence. It is consistent with the existing architecture and does not imply a new filename convention for future feature modules. Fixtures guide, Source audit notes and ADR 051 provide the supporting details.
docs/adr-019-modal-overlays.md:11:The old decision used a div overlay with `role="alertdialog"`, a focus trap written by hand and two buttons labeled Cancel and Discard. The overlay covered the page while keeping the underlying state in memory. This record describes that historical design; the replacement policy governs current dialog decisions.
docs/adr-019-modal-overlays.md:17:The custom trap moved Tab and Shift+Tab among the confirmation's controls. The dialog role and accessible name were intended to make the interruption understandable to assistive technology. Returning focus to the initiating control was part of the original interaction sketch, because leaving focus on a removed overlay would make the next keyboard step unpredictable.
docs/adr-019-modal-overlays.md:21:A div-based overlay allowed the same border, background and spacing choices as the rest of the page. It could be rendered conditionally with React without invoking a browser dialog method. The team expected that only two buttons and a short message would make the focus logic manageable.
docs/adr-019-modal-overlays.md:27:A focus trap is more than cycling between the first and last button in a static example. Controls can be removed, disabled or focused programmatically. Background content must not remain an alternate interaction path. Escape and dismissal behavior need coherent ownership. Styling an overlay is straightforward; duplicating browser dialog behavior consistently is a larger maintenance burden.
docs/adr-021-seeded-decks.md:25:`fresh-board.ts` uses browser cryptographic draws to select a valid seed and avoids the previous seed for a new deal. Once selected, the seed is just input to pure generation. Rendering frequency, animation and clock observations do not draw from the deck generator. Restart passes the existing code back through construction rather than drawing again.
docs/adr-021-seeded-decks.md:33:The tests therefore include literal vectors and rejection cases, not merely permutation checks. Pair counts and uniqueness are necessary but would not detect a changed draw order. Browser fixtures also use an independent oracle. The algorithm is small enough to inspect, yet its exact sequencing deserves strong evidence because it is part of the versioned format. See ADR 048 for the corresponding action-order decision.
