docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:1:# ADR 023: Present rankings as ordered lists
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:3:This historical record explains the retired ordered-list presentation for rankings and results.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:9:The early results sketch emphasized a phone-sized page. A sequence of entries could be stacked vertically, with the name and result together and little need for horizontal movement. At that point, the team chose ordered lists for rankings and results because they read well on phones.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:15:Each entry would have been one list row, with ordering carrying the ranking meaning. Supporting text could sit under the primary name without creating a wide grid. The list's native numbering appeared to provide a useful relationship between visual position and reading order.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:21:An ordered list can express a sequence without an additional position field in every visible row. It adapts naturally to wrapping text, and native list semantics are familiar to accessibility tools. With few fields, a list gives each entry enough vertical space without forcing a compact row height.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:29:Plain paragraphs were rejected as too weak a representation of order. A manually typed rank inside every paragraph would have reproduced part of an ordered list without its structural meaning. The initial list choice was therefore internally coherent for the information the sketch expected to emphasize.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:39:`GameStatus.tsx` uses a definition list for the current game's labeled statistics and a paragraph for completion. That is a different information shape from a ranked collection of participants. It should not be interpreted as an implementation of this old ordered-list decision or as evidence against the replacement page.
docs/adr-023-results-as-lists.md:41:Status bar, Tables policy and Interface review checklist are useful companions when reviewing actual screens. The superseding link at the top is the authority boundary: this historical rationale can explain a prototype, but it does not direct a new results presentation.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:1:# ADR 025: Provide a static reduced-motion presentation
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:3:This record explains the original reduced-motion commitment for card feedback and game completion.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:9:The first reduced-motion decision was to honor the browser's `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` setting for the game's visual feedback. The product retains static state changes while removing its card border transition and completion pulse under that preference. This record explains the accessibility reason; Motion policy holds the current motion guidance.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:13:`card.css` gives card borders a 120ms color transition. Hover changes a border, and matched state has its own green border. Under reduced motion the transition becomes `none`, so these states remain available without an interpolated change.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:15:`status.css` gives a played completion one 600ms pulse through the `.celebrate` class. Its reduced-motion media query sets animation to `none`. The completion paragraph still appears and remains visible. A player does not have to wait for a moving effect to learn the final moves or time.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:17:The mismatch interval is different. Showing two faces for 800 active milliseconds is a rule about observation time, not a decorative animation. The cards still close according to the same game transition. Removing the transition on a border does not change the mismatch countdown or the number of moves.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:21:The browser preference is already a user-controlled signal and works for the offline file without storing a second application setting. A game-level toggle would have duplicated that preference and added another persistence question. Treating motion as required feedback would also have made the reduced presentation less informative.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:23:The chosen effects are modest even in ordinary mode. The banner pulses once, and card hover changes only a border color. That restraint reduces distraction on large boards, where many animated objects would compete with the faces the player is trying to remember.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:27:Disabling all visual changes under reduced motion was never appropriate: revealing a face and marking a match are the game. Keeping every short effect because it is brief was also too weak a basis; short duration alone does not describe how an effect feels or whether it is necessary. The later historical exception in ADR 036 is superseded and does not govern the current implementation.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:29:A separate simplified theme could have mixed motion choices with color and geometry differences, making accessibility evidence harder to compare. The current media queries change the effects directly while preserving the same card shapes, layout and labels.
docs/adr-025-reduced-motion.md:33:`motion.e2e.test.ts` checks the ordinary durations and iteration count, then emulates reduced motion and verifies no card transition or victory animation. That is computed-style evidence from the built page, not an assumption that a media query exists somewhere in authored CSS.
