docs/adr-031-toast-announcements.md:3:This historical record explains the retired feature-owned live-region and toast announcement design.
docs/adr-031-toast-announcements.md:9:As the prototype gained turn results and reproduction messages, it was tempting to let each piece of UI own its feedback completely. The board could report matches, the import form could report loads and a later feature could publish its own result. This appeared to fit the principle of keeping behavior near its component.
docs/adr-031-toast-announcements.md:11:The old decision gave each feature its own `aria-live` region for messages, shown as a toast. A component would update its region when its operation completed and display the same message in a transient floating presentation. Announcements policy is the replacement authority.
docs/adr-031-toast-announcements.md:35:The current session has separate feedback text, load notice text and an announcement sequence. `Game.tsx` renders feedback in the game result area, and `Reproduction` keeps a visible load notice beside its controls. That arrangement should be read from the current policy and source, not reconstructed from the old per-feature toast idea.
docs/adr-033-mismatch-800ms.md:27:A CSS flip animation could visually hide the card after a delay, but it would not provide a reliable game state for input blocking, export or replay. It would also make reduced-motion handling interact with a rule that should be unchanged. The implementation instead keeps the observation interval in data and uses presentation only to show the resulting state.
