docs/adr-037-snapshot-pause.md:21:Resume has a special focus obligation because the button disappears. `Game.tsx` first calls `board.current?.focusRemembered()` and then invokes the controller's resume action. The controller resets its observation baseline and clears pause and load feedback. This order gives focus a surviving destination before removing the control that held it.
docs/adr-037-snapshot-pause.md:27:Immediate continuation would have been simpler but hostile to snapshots near a mismatch edge. Automatically closing the mismatch on import would have changed the saved progress. Restarting its full interval would have changed remaining active time. A separate modal inspection screen would have added another navigation and dialog problem to a small reproduction workflow.
docs/adr-002-typescript.md:13:Model game data with readonly interfaces and named discriminated unions. `SelectionKind` defines the string values and `Selection` connects each value to its associated fields. The switch in `activateCard` can therefore use `index` only in the one-card branch. Its unreachable branch accepts `never`, making an unhandled variant a useful compiler problem.
docs/adr-039-contrast-floor.md:13:Text readability, control boundaries, keyboard focus and face identity are related but not interchangeable. Main text uses `#F4F6FF`; secondary text uses `#B8BDD2`. Neutral borders use `#757C9A`. Focus is a 3px white outline offset by 2px. Those choices make the intended relationships concrete enough to inspect in the built page.
docs/adr-039-contrast-floor.md:19:A single ratio would not capture a clipped focus outline, a marker overlapping a square or matched cards faded until their faces become difficult to read. The current matched state stays at full opacity and adds both a green border and a check mark. Hover changes only the card border, so it does not dim or distort the face being remembered.
