docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:1:# ADR 041: Allow short names as typed
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:3:This historical record explains the retired one-to-thirty-two-character name limit and literal storage choice.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:9:The early name-entry discussion favored a small amount of validation. Names were expected to be short labels, not account identities, and the team wanted to avoid transforming a person's text unexpectedly. The initial rule allowed names with 1 to 32 characters and stored them exactly as typed.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:11:That choice belonged to an earlier interface design discussion. The game has no player-name entry field in its current control tree. Its text fields are a board code and snapshot JSON, with format-specific limits.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:15:The lower limit excluded an empty label. The upper limit was intended to keep an entry reasonably compact on a phone while allowing more than a very short nickname. Saving the original text was meant to preserve capitalization, spacing choices and the visible spelling a person had entered.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:17:The team treated name validation as a presentation concern rather than an identity-verification system. No account lookup or remote uniqueness check was involved. That matched the broader local-game approach: a label helped readers recognize an entry, but did not establish a trusted person behind a result.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:21:Transforming text can surprise people when the displayed result differs from the field they submitted. Literal storage looked easy to explain and easy to debug: the saved value would match the original draft. A modest length range appeared sufficient for a prototype with only a few entries.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:23:A larger range would have made wrapping and compact layouts harder to assess. A much smaller range risked excluding ordinary names without a clear gameplay reason. The original 32-character ceiling was a pragmatic visual estimate, not a result derived from the deck or scoring model.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:29:The literal-storage choice also avoided deciding which edits were harmless cleanup and which changed the user's intended name. That restraint had an appeal, but it left several text questions outside the simple range check: what counted as a character, how visually identical strings compared, and how invisible spacing affected display.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:33:The old decision's simplicity was incomplete. A numerical range and literal persistence did not by themselves define a predictable text-entry experience. The later policy handles the current name-length decision.
docs/adr-041-name-limits.md:39:The current board-code field has a 32-character control limit for a completely different reason: its canonical code syntax is bounded and validated by a parser. The snapshot textarea has its own much larger limit plus a UTF-8 byte check. Those limits should not be generalized from this retired name decision.
docs/adr-004-no-backend.md:19:`Preferences` contains a selected size and a map of best move counts. `rememberBest` compares a completed result with the entry for that size. This is a convenience for one browser's player, not an authority about play on other machines. The storage parser checks supported sizes and plausible bounded integer scores; it does not authenticate the person who produced them.
docs/adr-004-no-backend.md:21:That limitation was acceptable because the product does not depend on trusted remote standings. Treating local data as untrusted also helps ordinary reliability. A malformed preference value falls back to usable defaults instead of preventing a deal. An unavailable storage object leaves the current game playable. Persistence overview describes the operational detail.
docs/adr-004-no-backend.md:33:There is no remote recovery promise for an unexported in-memory game. A page reload starts a fresh deal, using a saved supported size when one is available. This follows the current session model rather than attempting to infer a cloud-save feature from local preferences. The absence of a backend also informs ADR 038, but that decision has its own reasons about what the game observes.
docs/adr-004-no-backend.md:37:The stored model is deliberately small. The adapter interface permits failure tests without turning the game core into a storage client.
docs/adr-043-keyboard-parity.md:31:Focused rule tests prove that an activation has one meaning. Browser input tests then prove that the supported controls reach that action and that focus movement does not. The complete accessibility journey starts with controls, enters a 4x4 board, exposes positions and face names, completes the board, leaves with Tab and starts again.
docs/adr-043-keyboard-parity.md:33:The tree record is automated evidence about available names and states. Spoken output with a screen reader remains acceptance work. Touch scrolling is tested in browser automation, while real-device long-press remains an explicit manual observation. These limits keep the parity claim tied to the documented actions rather than every conceivable gesture.
docs/adr-006-native-controls.md:19:`BoardCodeForm` uses a real form. Submission reads `boardCode` through `FormData`, prevents navigation and asks the controller to validate it. A successful replacement remounts the code input with the new generation; the submit button remains available as the focus destination. Invalid input keeps the draft available for correction.
docs/adr-006-native-controls.md:27:Native does not mean unstyled. Shared controls use the panel background and border tokens, and keyboard focus has a visible outline. Card sizing and face rendering are specific to the game. The decision concerns the foundation beneath that presentation, not a promise to preserve every browser default margin.
docs/adr-006-native-controls.md:31:A native button does not automatically make a board easy to navigate. The project still implements a remembered tab stop, positional names, nearest scrolling and turn feedback. Browser tests check those behaviors against the built game. The complete keyboard journey includes leaving the board and starting another game, so evidence extends beyond pressing Enter on a single isolated element.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:9:This was a scope choice. The small product could give its current labels, accessible names and acceptance journey one coherent review instead of shipping partially translated controls. It was not a claim that memory games are culturally neutral or that translating visible strings alone would make the whole experience localized.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:13:Deal and reproduction labels are named constants in `controls/control-labels.ts`. Status labels live in `status/status-labels.ts`. Session notices and turn-result text live with session behavior. Face names come from ordered design data, and card position/state labels are constructed in `card-state.ts`.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:15:These locations make the current vocabulary findable without pretending that it is already a translation system. A string constructed from pair count still needs correct grammar; the first matched pair and later pairs use different forms. A face label has a specified order of palette, fill, shape and marker language. Voice guide and Glossary explain the current terms.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:19:Board codes and JSON field names are versioned data syntax. Their meaning does not change because interface text is English. The parser expects the stated code form and exact JSON keys. Localizing a button label would be a presentation change; translating serialized field names would change a format contract.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:21:Likewise, the deck stores numeric face IDs rather than English names. `faceDesign` supplies the name for presentation. That distinction keeps the pure pair comparison independent of the wording a player hears or reads. Pair identity and Format versioning guide cover these boundaries.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:25:A translation framework from the start would have provided extraction and lookup machinery but no reviewed translations by itself. Embedding placeholder catalogues could imply broader support than the product had evidence for. Relying on automatic browser translation would leave accessible names and exact instructional language outside the team's reviewed behavior.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:27:The accepted choice was explicit English scope with ordinary source organization. This avoids committing to an unimplemented locale-detection algorithm, fallback language sequence or translation file layout. Those would be new product and architecture decisions, not consequences that can be inferred from the existing label constants.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:31:Players who need another language do not receive a supported localized interface in this version. That is a real limitation. The current automated accessibility journey checks English names and messages, so it cannot be cited as evidence for translated speech or layout.
docs/adr-045-no-i18n-yet.md:35:The title, help text, controls and completion summary remain in one page, and current wrapping is tested at constrained viewports. ADR 049, Typography and Screen reader notes describe that English presentation evidence. The present decision is to document what is supported accurately rather than infer a multilingual feature from an otherwise language-independent matching rule.
