docs/adr-008-seven-sizes.md:9:Even square sides make every board contain an even number of positions. Each face can occur twice without a spare card, and row-order indices can use one side length for both navigation axes. This does not make other shapes impossible; it keeps the current product's rules and controls compact.
docs/adr-008-seven-sizes.md:13:The sizes cover 16 through 256 cards, or 8 through 128 pairs. The intermediate choices provide a meaningful increase in memory load without requiring a new mode. A 6x6 board has 18 pairs; an 8x8 has 32; 10x10 has 50; 12x12 has 72; 14x14 has 98. The same reveal, match and mismatch behavior applies throughout.
docs/adr-008-seven-sizes.md:21:Selecting a new size asks the controller for a fresh deal at that size. A successful board-code or snapshot load updates the selected size to match the loaded game. A failed load leaves the active deal in place. This creates a consistent meaning for the selector: it describes the board currently being played, not merely a preference for the next visit. See Board size catalogue and New game and restart.
docs/adr-047-touch-scroll.md:3:This record explains completed-click activation and the focus-scrolling order that preserves pointer and touch input.
docs/adr-010-snapshot-json.md:19:Positions are zero-based row-order indices. Export sorts matched positions so equivalent sets have a stable readable ordering. Active time can retain fractions in memory; export floors elapsed milliseconds and rounds pending mismatch time upward. Rounding the remaining interval upward keeps a still-open mismatch from becoming a zero-duration serialized state.
docs/adr-010-snapshot-json.md:37:The encoder names its public fields explicitly instead of stringifying the entire GameState object.
docs/adr-012-no-sound.md:13:A matched card stays face up, receives a green border and displays a check mark. A mismatch remains visible for its active interval before both cards close. The status area shows moves, pair progress and elapsed time. A completed board has a visible summary. None of those observations requires a sound effect to explain what happened.
docs/adr-012-no-sound.md:31:A celebratory experience is carried by the banner and its short permitted pulse. The reduced-motion presentation remains understandable because the summary text and matched board stay visible. Removing motion does not reveal an audio dependency that was concealed during ordinary play. See ADR 025 and ADR 050.
