docs/adr-053-docs-in-repo.md:25:A page that says a behavior exists is weaker than the source and tests when they disagree. The current game can be inspected through `Game.tsx`, the session adapter and pure core. Documentation names those real boundaries so a reader can verify an account instead of relying on a vague architectural diagram.
docs/adr-053-docs-in-repo.md:31:The documentation set links related pages by filename. A reader can move from board-code syntax to seeded generation, from snapshot validation to import pause, or from large-board layout to focus scrolling. Links are most useful when they lead to a different level of explanation rather than repeat the same paragraph on several pages.
docs/adr-014-pause-on-hidden.md:13:`useSessionClock` keeps a visibility reference initialized from `document.hidden`. When a `visibilitychange` event arrives, it synchronizes using the previous visibility value, then updates visibility and reschedules. The ordering matters. Hiding first credits the final visible slice; returning first discards the hidden slice. Reversing those operations would charge or omit the wrong interval.
docs/adr-057-small-components.md:7:A single game component could contain the select, status, 256 buttons, snapshot editor, timers and storage effects. That would be quick to start and difficult to review once input, import and visibility interact. The project instead separates the view by behavior while keeping the composition easy to follow.
docs/adr-057-small-components.md:15:This arrangement prevents a card from reading storage or deciding whether a snapshot load should pause. It also prevents the controller from querying every button to paint its revealed state. Each layer can be understood in terms of the data or browser effect it actually owns. React boundaries explains those connections.
docs/adr-057-small-components.md:31:A monolithic component would reduce imports but mix unrelated reasons to change. Splitting every few lines into a new component would create indirection without a stable behavior boundary. A generic reusable-control framework could add configuration for cases this game does not have. The chosen middle ground uses small named pieces with concrete jobs.
docs/adr-057-small-components.md:50:  const matchedCards = useMemo(() => new Set(matched), [matched]);
docs/adr-057-small-components.md:51:  // The game passes a new handler on each render. Cards keep one callback that calls the latest.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:15:Local presentation state stays nearer its use. The focus hook remembers an index in the board. The snapshot textarea lives in `Reproduction`. The code-input draft remains in its native input until a game generation replaces it. These pieces do not need a global subscription channel because no unrelated feature is consuming them.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:19:`useGame` pairs React state with a `current` ref. Its `commit` function updates the ref immediately and asks React to render the new session. Clock synchronization and successive actions consult the ref, so they operate on the latest committed session rather than a value captured by an earlier render.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:21:This matters when an action arrives between scheduled clock observations. The controller synchronizes, obtains the current session and then applies activation. It does not dispatch time and input to separate stores that might observe them in a different order. ADR 013 explains why synchronization precedes activation.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:29:Putting every transition directly inside `Game.tsx` was the opposite alternative. It would have reduced file count while mixing DOM composition, clock observation, storage and turn rules. The chosen controller boundary keeps those responsibilities legible without building a general application framework.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:33:The controller has to be deliberate about action ordering and stale callbacks. The session generation is part of that discipline: replacement increments it, and ticks from another generation do nothing. Focus memory resets through the board's React key rather than an event broadcast. These mechanisms are small enough to inspect and have focused tests.
docs/adr-016-no-state-library.md:35:Format helpers serve both snapshot and replay validation; the board computes face-up positions for rendering. The reason to share is a concrete common meaning, not an expectation that every future feature needs a store. See Architecture, ADR 057 and Game state model.
docs/announcements-policy.md:5:The game has many visible updates, but only some represent a new result. A clock tick is a changing value; resolving a turn is an event. Treating those as the same kind of update would make a memory game noisy and would bury the information a player needs after selecting two cards.
docs/announcements-policy.md:9:`Game.tsx` places the game result between the board instructions and the Board itself. Its paragraph has `role="status"`, `aria-live="polite"` and `aria-atomic="true"`, with the current feedback inside a span. The page has exactly one live region, the status paragraph in Game.tsx. New status text, such as whose turn it is, goes through that region. The current game publishes pair results and load outcomes there; it has no turn-owner feature.
docs/announcements-policy.md:38:A card's accessible name also changes when it reveals or matches. That is the focused control describing its own state, not a second turn-result producer. The text in the event paragraph summarizes what the two-card attempt achieved. Screen reader notes follows those two sources in a complete game.
docs/announcements-policy.md:42:Two misses in a row produce the same sentence. Updating only a string state can leave the DOM text unchanged, which gives no new mutation for the second event. The keyed span avoids relying on a different visible sentence to distinguish them.
docs/announcements-policy.md:52:`tickSession` advances the game and returns a new session with that game, leaving feedback and announcement fields intact. Mismatch closure is part of this advancement. It does not publish a new “cards closed” result. The earlier mismatch sentence already told the player that closure would follow a short pause.
docs/announcements-policy.md:54:Arrow movement is local board focus state. It does not call the session feedback path. Clock renders update Time in GameStatus, while card memoization prevents unchanged cards from rendering again. Neither operation is a new turn result.
docs/announcements-policy.md:62:The controller's `notice(feedback)` helper assigns both `feedback` and `loadNotice`, then increments `announcement`. Invalid Board code publishes `Invalid board code.`; invalid Snapshot JSON publishes `Invalid snapshot.`. Repeating the same invalid submission is a new load attempt and advances the identity again.
docs/announcements-policy.md:76:A completed snapshot instead publishes its load outcome, because import is the event that just occurred. The game is visibly complete, but no new card match happened. This distinction preserves a useful account of what the user actually did.
docs/adr-018-playwright.md:23:The browser clock is controlled for timing edges. Visibility tests explicitly drive the browser adapter's visibility input because headless tabs do not necessarily behave like interactive desktop tabs. That limitation is documented in the test rather than concealed behind a test name claiming to have physically switched applications.
docs/adr-018-playwright.md:27:The named compatibility combination is recorded as Node 26.12.1, npm 11.20.0, Playwright 1.64.1 and Chromium 156.0.8078.4. The wrapper writes the runtime it actually used and can require that named combination through its verification flag. The README records results for that combination; it does not establish results for every browser family.
docs/adr-018-playwright.md:29:Accessibility-tree checks cover a complete keyboard journey, names, states and feedback. They are not a record of spoken output from a screen reader. The README explicitly records that a screen reader was not run. Real-device long-press context-menu behavior likewise remains a user-acceptance observation. Accessibility audit keeps those distinctions visible.
