docs/corrupt-data-policy.md:5:Browser storage contains strings outside the type system. A value may have been written by an older build, edited by a person, truncated by a debugging experiment or replaced by another script on the origin. The current preference reader accepts only a usable preference aggregate and otherwise returns its default. It does not try to repair individual score entries.
docs/corrupt-data-policy.md:93:In the browser, those latter two stages are separated by React's render and effect cycle rather than by a player's explicit action. `useGame` initializes session state from the read result, then `useSavedPreferences` writes that state after mounting. A test inspecting storage only after the UI has settled can see valid defaults and miss the original corrupt text entirely.
docs/corrupt-data-policy.md:101:Stored preference rejection happens during initialization with no draft to correct in the game UI. It returns a playable state quietly. There is no recovery dialog, corrupted-data banner or download of rejected text. Browser access failure also returns defaults, but concerns operation availability rather than text acceptance; see Storage failure policy.
docs/corrupt-data-policy.md:107:`storage.test.ts` already covers malformed JSON, null, an empty object, unsupported size, scores below the pair count, negative and fractional values, and a noncanonical key. It separately checks that a score at the move cap round-trips and one beyond it is rejected. The browser persistence contract test seeds malformed text before navigation and checks for a playable 16-card default board.
docs/corrupt-data-policy.md:109:Those tests are focused evidence, not an exhaustive enumeration of every possible JSON string. A new bug report is easiest to isolate with its exact raw value and one field changed at a time. Comparing an independently written valid baseline with the failing value exposes whether the issue concerns syntax, object shape, selected size, key spelling or score bounds. Storage testing guide gives the browser setup and store doubles for doing that without modifying the source.
docs/adr-020-vitest.md:13:Deck tests cover composition, literal generation vectors and rejection sampling. Game tests cover the first reveal, second reveal, ignored activation and completion. Snapshot tests challenge exact fields and internal relationships. Replay tests verify ordered logical actions and time boundaries. The session tests cover state replacement, pause and result feedback without needing React to render.
docs/dependency-policy.md:5:The source of truth for installed package names and versions is `package.json` together with `package-lock.json`. Technology specifies the stack and pinned tools; the manifest also contains formatting and image-analysis tools used by this implementation. The distinction matters when investigating a lockfile change: the complete current manifest is broader than the technology page's main stack table.
docs/dependency-policy.md:9:The runtime dependency set is React and React DOM. Build, type checking, tests, formatting and image analysis sit in development tooling. A feature adds no entries to dependencies or devDependencies. Existing packages cover rendering, native control interaction, deterministic rules, build delivery and the verification tools described below.
docs/dependency-policy.md:11:This is an application with a private package manifest, not a published library. Its package name is `neon-pairs-reference`, version `1.0.0`, with `private: true` and `type: module`. The engine field accepts Node `>=24.19.0`. Those facts describe the project package; they do not mean the Node runtime becomes part of the distributed HTML.
docs/dependency-policy.md:36:React state and effects are sufficient for the current session. `use-game.ts` stores one `Session` and a ref to its latest value, then returns named actions. There is no state-management package coordinating the core. Components get data and functions through props, and the board keeps focus locally.
docs/dependency-policy.md:40:The deterministic generator is plain TypeScript. Snapshot and replay readers use JSON and explicit validation. Timing uses a browser adapter plus numerical deltas. Preferences use a narrow storage interface. These implementations are small enough to inspect against the specification, which is especially valuable where a package's general behavior would be broader than the game's contract.
docs/dependency-policy.md:56:The repository uses Playwright's Chromium API and assertions under Node's test runner. The browser binary is a separate installation from the locked JavaScript package. The browser helper records actual versions and can enforce the README's named combination; Build pipeline explains that evidence and the executable override.
docs/dependency-policy.md:64:The audit explicitly asserts that the sorted runtime dependency names are `react` and `react-dom`. For the reviewed version fixture, it compares installed manifest values by name. It does not recursively prove the behavior of every transitive package in the lockfile, and its source is not a vulnerability scanner. Those limits are useful when interpreting a passing result.
docs/dependency-policy.md:72:These are different kinds of evidence. Unit transitions help with rules, but they cannot show that a bundle opens from disk without requests. A successful type check cannot show that the browser used the named executable. A screenshot cannot show that the lockfile and manifest agree. Test policy maps those checks to their actual responsibilities.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:3:This record explains positional card names, revealed face names and the boundary around hidden identity.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:9:The chosen accessible name combines position with the card's current state. A closed card exposes its one-based row and column and the word “closed.” A revealed or matched card adds the face description. `describeCard` in `card-state.ts` is the small function that keeps this naming logic together.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:13:Internal positions are zero-based row-order indices. The label converts them to a row and column using division and remainder by the board size. The first closed card is “Row 1, column 1, closed.” This coordinate language stays stable when the board scrolls or the player changes input method.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:19:`faceDesign` constructs the face label from palette name, fill, shape and marker direction. Face 0 is “cyan solid circle, marker top”; face 64 is the corresponding outline circle. The actual card label places the state before that face description. Both artwork and text come from the same ordered design data.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:27:This boundary makes names part of the game model presented to a player, not an accessibility-only back door. Tests know the deal through its documented code and independent generation; the application does not need a solver hook to support testing.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:31:Short names such as “Card 7” would identify a button but give less spatial structure for board navigation. A separate accessible image description would duplicate the button's meaning and risk becoming an extra navigable object. Describing every card with its face from initial load would make the game a different task for different users.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:35:Unit tests check representative closed, revealed and matched names, and browser tests inspect the accessibility tree through a full keyboard game. Spoken output remains an acceptance check with the user's screen reader. ADR 018, ADR 024 and ADR 043 cover the neighboring evidence and input decisions.
docs/adr-022-card-names.md:37:## The name constructor
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:1:# Duplicate names policy
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:3:This page explains the group-level name comparison policy and the distinctions a reviewer needs when checking it.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:5:The current source has no name list or group editor. A board code identifies a deal, a card label identifies a position and face state, and a local best is indexed by board size. None of those is a collection of person-entered names. The policy below belongs to a feature that introduces such a collection.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:9:The group being edited supplies the comparison scope; the draft alone cannot establish whether it repeats an existing entry. The candidate's accepted representation comes from Text entry policy. Names in one group compare without regard to case, after normalization. A repeat blocks the action and shows "Each name must be different." This lets the same visible spelling with different capitalization be recognized as a repeat without requiring that all displayed names be rewritten in one case.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:11:The scope is one group. Neon Pairs has no global name registry across devices, browser origins or unrelated groups. A local comparison can answer whether an entry repeats within the group being acted on without implying that the person owns a unique account name.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:13:The check concerns names, rather than board positions or face identities. Two cards intentionally share a face, and a replay can intentionally activate the same position more than once. Those repetitions follow their own game rules. A generic “remove duplicates everywhere” helper would not express this policy's subject or scope.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:17:Case-insensitive comparison does not by itself prescribe changing a name's displayed capitalization. `Mira` and `mira` can compare as the same candidate while the accepted display spelling remains the one chosen by the person who entered it. Showing a lowercased comparison key as the name would conflate two purposes.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:19:A useful implementation trace therefore distinguishes the value displayed, the value accepted into the group and whatever representation is used to compare it. The comparison representation is an internal tool. It is not a new user-visible identifier, a format field or a replacement for the accepted name.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:21:The policy does not specify locale selection or a particular Unicode case-folding API. That matters for names whose case behavior differs across languages or scripts. Name normalization retains diacritics and does not transliterate names. Examples with ordinary Latin letters illustrate the unambiguous case while case behavior across Unicode varies.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:27:| Existing accepted name | Candidate | What the example demonstrates |
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:30:| `Mira` | `mira` | Capitalization does not distinguish the names |
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:32:| `Ada Lovelace` | The same name after representation processing | The comparison uses accepted name text |
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:35:These cases are illustrations of the policy rather than a complete Unicode test corpus. They also do not decide length validity; Name length policy covers that independent check. A candidate can satisfy length and still repeat an existing name.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:37:A group with several entries can expose a bug that a two-entry example misses. If only the immediately preceding name is checked, a repeat of the first entry can slip through after a different middle entry. The policy speaks about the group, so the relevant evidence is membership across that group rather than adjacency in the rendered form.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:41:A repeated candidate does not become an accepted additional name as part of the failed action. The exact feedback above explains why acceptance stopped. Automatically adding a suffix, changing capitalization or deleting one of the existing names would implement a different outcome from the one stated.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:45:The current reproduction handlers demonstrate an explicit acceptance boundary: parse first, replace only on success, otherwise publish a notice. A name feature can be reviewed with the same question—where does a candidate become accepted?—without reusing the board parser or claiming that load errors are name errors. Error display guide provides the current feedback mechanics.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:49:Because no name feature exists, the existing tests do not establish this policy. A screenshot containing distinct visible names would be weak evidence even for a future feature: the comparison bug might only appear on submission, on an edit or after a change in capitalization.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:51:Useful examples include an exact repeat, a case-only repeat, a repeat that is not adjacent in the displayed list and a different accepted name. An edit scenario reveals whether the current entry is being confused with another entry. A separate-group example reveals whether an implementation accidentally enforces a broader scope than the policy states.
docs/duplicate-names-policy.md:53:For a defect report, the accepted group values, candidate value, action and observed message make the comparison reproducible. Display order can be recorded as context, but it does not define identity. The policy stays about equality within the group, and its representation and length dependencies remain in their own linked pages.
docs/adr-024-roving-focus.md:7:A 16x16 board has 256 card buttons. Putting all of them into the page's ordinary Tab order would make reaching the reproduction controls or leaving the board laborious. Removing the board from the keyboard path would be worse. The selected compromise is one remembered tab stop inside the board, with arrow keys for spatial movement.
docs/adr-024-roving-focus.md:21:`Game.tsx` keys the board with the session generation. A successful new deal, Restart or load replaces the board's focus memory and starts again at the first position. That reset does not itself move the browser's focus away from the action control. It changes where a later Tab entry goes.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:4:The production interface has very little iconography. Card identities are drawn by `Face.tsx`, matched state uses a small SVG check, and hidden cards use a bordered span rotated into a diamond. Action controls retain visible text. This follows the DOM-button and inline-SVG approach in Visuals V01 and the face geometry in V10 through V16.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:10:| Hidden-card diamond | `card.css` | 12px span, 2px border, static rotation |
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:41:The check is not the text character `✓`. Its appearance does not depend on whether the system UI font contains that glyph or how it aligns with surrounding text. The state word lives in the card's accessible name, while the visual check remains hidden from the accessibility tree.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:45:A closed card contains an empty span with a border and a 45-degree transform. The transform is static; it does not spin or animate on reveal. Its border uses the secondary text color, and its size is constant across identities. Every closed card therefore has the same decoration.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:47:Using a CSS border here keeps the back small and neutral without loading a face SVG or looking up a glyph. It also avoids confusing a closed card's diamond decoration with an actual revealed diamond identity. The latter is a central SVG path accompanied by its marker and fill mode.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:49:Because global border-box sizing applies, the span's 12px width includes its border. Rotation increases its axis-aligned visible footprint. A reviewer comparing it with the face diamond needs to compare the actual rendered geometry rather than two nominal dimensions from different coordinate systems.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:53:Top-level actions and reproduction controls render their visible names as ordinary text inside native buttons. The size selector renders textual options. There is no icon-only replacement for New game, Restart, loading or exporting in the game UI. This leaves the action's wording available without interpreting a symbolic convention.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:55:A decorative SVG inside a card uses `aria-hidden="true"` and `focusable="false"`. The button supplies its own accessible name. Giving the internal SVG a separate title would change that accessibility structure; the current renderer avoids duplicated face descriptions by naming the button once through `describeCard`.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:57:The face sheet does show generated names as captions, because it is an inspection tool. Those captions are ordinary system text outside the cards. They do not change the face geometry or imply that every game card should display its full name visually. Typography describes this separation of page text from card art.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:73:An external illustration or icon-font specimen would not be a suitable replacement for that fixture's evidence. It could have correct-looking symbols while omitting the card border, interior size, marker surround and matched overlay. The existing fixture's value is that it brings those constraints into the preview together.
docs/icon-and-glyph-policy.md:79:A shape-name change also reaches the generated accessible description through the ordered table. A path-only edit can change appearance while leaving names stable. Keeping those two effects visible in review helps detect a card that looks like one shape while being described as another.
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:7:The current game has one dark appearance. The page background is `#090B14`, panels use `#141827`, closed cards use `#1B2034` and face-up cards use `#111625`. Main and supporting text have separate light colors, borders are neutral and focus is white. The root declares `color-scheme: dark` so native controls participate in the intended appearance.
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:13:The background layers distinguish the page, containers and card states without replacing the face identity system. A hidden card has the same neutral diamond back regardless of its future face. A revealed face has a slightly different surface, while matched cards add the green border and check mark. The palette therefore supports state recognition instead of becoming another matching rule.
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:15:Artwork colors are a separate ordered palette. Shape, marker and fill distinguish identities; color reinforces the marker direction. That makes the faces usable when hue distinctions are unavailable. Color policy and ADR 007 explain the relationship.
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:21:There is no theme picker in the controls and no theme field in `Preferences`. This keeps the saved model focused on the selected board size and best moves. A browser preference for reduced motion is handled separately because it concerns effects rather than selecting a second palette.
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:54:  --border: #757c9a;
docs/adr-026-dark-theme.md:60:  box-sizing: border-box;
docs/module-layout-policy.md:11:The session directory has a different responsibility. It connects rules to React state, browser observations and saved preferences. A feature lives in src/game/FEATURE/, and its rules live in src/game/FEATURE/rules.ts, which imports neither React nor any browser global. That placement gives a reader a predictable starting point for a feature's decisions, with the integration code close enough to inspect alongside them.
docs/module-layout-policy.md:13:This convention applies to feature work; existing behaviors also use other filenames. The current core is in `game.ts`, `deck.ts`, `selection.ts`, `snapshot.ts`, `snapshot-selection.ts`, `saved-format.ts`, `elapsed-time.ts` and `replay.ts` directly under `src/game`. The board already uses focused names such as `keyboard.ts` and `card-state.ts`.
docs/module-layout-policy.md:21:There are intentional lateral imports. `storage.ts` uses `GRID_SIZES` and `MOVES_MAX` to validate preferences against actual game limits. `card-state.ts` uses `faceDesign` to describe a visible face. These relationships connect meanings that genuinely belong together. They do not form a general helpers layer with unrelated operations.
docs/module-layout-policy.md:49:The source convention tests also inspect exported values. They look for an importer for every recognized export and a single owner for each recognized exported constant name. This is a lightweight check, not a complete module system. Its practical effect is to discourage speculative public helpers and ambiguous exported constants. Source audit notes describes the exact text patterns and their limitations.
docs/module-layout-policy.md:67:A correction to arrow-key edge behavior belongs first in `board/keyboard.ts`, which computes a target index from an index, size, key and modifiers. Its React caller translates a target into DOM focus. A change to the scroll behavior belongs in the hook or `nearest-scroll.ts`, because it concerns the browser's focus and scrolling behavior. Neither change calls for a new game-state field.
docs/module-layout-policy.md:71:A turn-count defect belongs in `activateCard`. The displayed statistic and exported snapshot already consume its result. Fixing the number in `GameStatus` would leave replay, completion and storage observing the old value. These examples are consequences of the existing structure, not extra parallel implementations.
docs/adr-028-prettier.md:15:Readable diffs are particularly useful here because many important changes are small. Moving synchronization before an activation or changing an exact bound can alter behavior without changing much text. A formatter reduces unrelated visual noise around that change. It cannot determine whether the ordering or number is correct.
docs/name-length-policy.md:3:This page defines how the name-length check is interpreted and separates it from native text-field limits.
docs/name-length-policy.md:5:The current game has no person-name field or name validator. Its existing text limits belong to Board code and Snapshot JSON. They are useful examples of browser entry constraints, but neither is an implementation of the name policy on this page.
docs/name-length-policy.md:9:The length check concerns the accepted name representation, after the processing described in Text entry policy. Counting the raw draft instead can produce a different result from counting the value that the feature will store and display. A name has 2 to 20 Unicode code points after normalization. Anything else blocks the action and shows "Use 2 to 20 characters." This check therefore belongs at the point where a draft would become an accepted name, rather than being inferred from the field's apparent width.
docs/name-length-policy.md:11:The message is the user-facing explanation of this failure. The numerical bounds are inclusive: the endpoints pass, and values outside them fail. The policy does not define an exception for a one-character name, a separate limit for a family name or an alternate bound for another script.
docs/name-length-policy.md:13:A failure blocks the action that is trying to accept the name. It is not a request to silently cut the entered value to fit. Truncating a longer string would accept a different name and would conceal which action failed. The policy specifies feedback and blocking rather than a repair that guesses the user's intended spelling.
docs/name-length-policy.md:23:In JavaScript, iterating a string by code point is different from indexing its individual UTF-16 units. `Array.from(value).length` or a spread-based count illustrates that distinction for well-formed text. This is an explanatory example, not a requirement to allocate an array in a particular implementation. The source has no name helper.
docs/name-length-policy.md:41:“Passes” here means that the length condition is satisfied. It does not imply that an action with other applicable checks is guaranteed to succeed. The Duplicate names policy addresses a separate group-level condition without changing the unit used by this check.
docs/name-length-policy.md:43:## The order is observable
docs/name-length-policy.md:45:A raw draft may have surrounding or repeated spacing that will not be retained in its accepted form. Counting before the representation step can reject a value that would fit after processing, or accept a value whose later transformations were not what the length helper expected. The policy's ordering avoids having the stored name disagree with the checked name.
docs/name-length-policy.md:47:A reviewer can follow one candidate from field submission to accepted model and then display. The decisive value is the same one used for the count. An implementation that checks one temporary string but saves a different raw draft has two representations and cannot establish the stated condition simply by having a correctly named length function.
docs/name-length-policy.md:53:There is no name-length test in the repository. Keyboard and reproduction checks establish native field labels, draft preservation and load outcomes. They do not verify the name count merely because they type strings into controls.
docs/name-length-policy.md:55:When this policy is implemented, its evidence can name the processed candidate and observed outcome at the boundary. That is enough to distinguish a counting defect from a display problem or another group-level validation failure. The policy stays narrow: one accepted value, one counting unit, one inclusive interval and the stated blocking message.
docs/adr-030-move-cap.md:7:Moves increase on a second eligible reveal. In normal play the count stays small, but imported progress and long-lived sessions make the numeric boundary part of the product. Leaving it unbounded would make storage validation and display behavior less explicit. Treating a high count as game over would add a failure mode unrelated to the goal of finding every pair.
docs/adr-030-move-cap.md:17:## Format and storage boundaries
docs/adr-030-move-cap.md:35:The visible best remains a per-size move record. Reaching a display boundary does not add points, create a new scoring mode or remove the need to finish all pairs. Win conditions remains the definition of completion.
docs/retention-policy.md:3:This page explains retention for saved feature lists and contrasts it with the compact best-score aggregate in the current game.
docs/retention-policy.md:5:Retention describes how much accepted data remains after a save. It is separate from parsing a malformed value, clearing site data in browser settings or losing a private browsing session. The game currently remembers one best move count per supported size rather than preserving individual completed attempts. A browser dump of those bests cannot reconstruct the order or number of games played.
docs/retention-policy.md:9:A list can grow as a feature saves additional records, even when every individual record is small. Its retention boundary applies during saving, rather than acting as an expiration clock while the player is idle. A stored list keeps its newest 10 records and drops older ones when a new record is saved. This bounds the retained collection while leaving the active interaction independent of an ever-growing history.
docs/retention-policy.md:11:This rule concerns a saved list. It does not turn the existing `bestMoves` object into a history or add a timestamp to it. Record types, sort fields and tie-breaking conventions are separate from the amount retained. The preference store implements the best-score aggregate rather than a feature list.
docs/retention-policy.md:29:This makes the data easy to explain: a valid best remains available across normal reloads until another relevant event changes the storage or the value is rejected. A better result can replace it. Browser policy or a person's site-data action can remove it. Another tab can overwrite the aggregate. None of those is an application age policy.
docs/retention-policy.md:31:The distinction matters when comparing evidence from two visits. A gap between visits does not by itself identify the cause of a missing best. If the origin changed, the second visit may have read another storage area. If the value was edited into an invalid shape, the reader may have used its fallback. If the browser cleared data, the key may simply be absent. Storage quota notes discusses environment-driven loss without treating it as list pruning.
docs/retention-policy.md:35:The preference writer runs from a React effect whose dependencies are the preferences object and store. This is not a page-exit flush. On initial mounting, the effect writes the current preference state. Later, a new preference object causes another effect run. There is no dependency on active elapsed milliseconds or every move in the game.
docs/retention-policy.md:37:`replaceGame` always constructs a preference object with the new game's size. New Game and Restart consequently pass through a save-capable path even when the size value stays the same. Valid board-code and snapshot loads do so as well. Invalid loads update feedback without replacing preferences.
docs/retention-policy.md:39:`rememberBest` returns the original object for equal or worse results. The session can still render a completed game, but the preference identity need not change. This keeps the storage effect aligned with meaningful preference updates in that path. It is not a universal deduplication layer: replacement actions can still write the same serialized preference contents again.
docs/retention-policy.md:41:For list work, the save event named by the retention rule should be understood in the feature's own operation. The existing preference effect has no generic event log or timer that scans all browser keys. There is no central list manager in this source tree.
docs/retention-policy.md:45:The app has no account or service that copies the aggregate elsewhere. A best kept in one profile remains local to that browser's storage context. Opening the same HTML in another browser does not fetch it. Exporting a snapshot copies game progress, not a backup of the browser's accumulated bests or selected-size history.
docs/retention-policy.md:47:The UI has no general preference export, browser-data restore or clear-records control. A developer can inspect or change storage manually, but those are debugging actions rather than product controls.
docs/retention-policy.md:51:A bounded collection makes its record count predictable. It does not guarantee every write succeeds: browser access can be blocked before capacity matters, and another application on the same origin may consume storage. A small value can fail to write even though its own size is well below a browser's nominal limit.
docs/retention-policy.md:53:Conversely, a successful write does not prove indefinite lifetime. The browser can later clear data, a private context can end, or the person can remove site storage. Those environmental conditions are outside the saved-list retention operation. Private browsing notes describes how a value can survive a reload yet disappear when the browsing session ends.
docs/retention-policy.md:57:## Checking retention with feature records
docs/retention-policy.md:59:The existing test suite establishes preference behavior rather than a list API. `storage.test.ts` checks independent minimums for two sizes and object identity for equal and worse results. Browser journeys check that completed bests survive reloads and game resets. None of those tests asserts a sequence of saved feature records.
docs/retention-policy.md:61:For a feature-list review, concrete before-and-after examples can expose the retention boundary without adding fields to the product format. Records can be identified by the already defined domain representation in that feature's tests. The useful observation is which accepted records are present after a save, compared with the preceding accepted collection. Examples can use the feature's existing record fields.
docs/retention-policy.md:63:It is also useful to keep failed writes separate from successful retention checks. A denied store can leave the feature's in-memory state ahead of the browser value. Inspecting the old browser string then proves only that the write failed, not that the retention calculation chose the wrong records. Storage testing guide describes the store doubles that make this separation observable.
docs/retention-policy.md:67:The best map cannot answer how many attempts occurred, which deal produced a best, how long that attempt took, or whether two equal results happened on different days. None of those fields is retained. A new feature that needs a collection has a different persistence concern from calculating a size-specific minimum.
docs/adr-032-best-score-scope.md:13:`Preferences.bestMoves` is a readonly record keyed by canonical side-length strings. `rememberBest` obtains `String(size)`, compares the existing number and returns either the original preferences object or a new map with one changed entry. The equality case is deliberately a no-op rather than a rewrite with identical data.
docs/adr-032-best-score-scope.md:15:`activateSession` calls that helper when a player action completes the board. The pure game core knows how to recognize a win, but does not write a browser record. Storage remains an adapter concern. This allows a game transition to be replayed or validated without requiring a local storage object.
docs/adr-032-best-score-scope.md:23:A weighted score combining time and moves would have needed a new formula and an explanation of why one second was worth some fraction of a turn. A percentage-of-perfect score could compare sizes, but it would obscure the direct count a player already sees. Neither was needed for the local record behavior.
docs/adr-032-best-score-scope.md:35:Focused tests cover better, equal and worse results and the storage validator's bounds. Browser tests complete games, reload and verify the selected size's displayed record. That combination checks the comparison itself and its persistence path. ADR 030, Scoring and moves and Persistence overview are the related references.
docs/tables-policy.md:3:This page records the markup policy for ordered result data and explains why the current game statistics use a different structure.
docs/tables-policy.md:5:There is no result table in the current UI. `GameStatus.tsx` displays four properties of a single game in a definition list. Reproduction controls use forms, and the board is a CSS grid of buttons. None of those structures is evidence that ordered records have already been implemented.
docs/tables-policy.md:11:This is a content-specific policy. It does not make the game board a data table because it has rows and columns, and it does not require converting every compact summary to tabular markup. The distinction begins with what the content means: interactive card coordinates, a property's value and a record in an ordered result collection are different things.
docs/tables-policy.md:13:No such result feature is present in `Game.tsx`. The policy applies when result data is introduced. Native controls guide discusses the broader preference for native semantics without adding another result format.
docs/tables-policy.md:17:GameStatus renders Moves, Pairs, Time and Best as four `dt`/`dd` groups inside a `dl`. Each term names a property of the same current game. There are no peer result rows and no column whose meaning changes with the row being read.
docs/tables-policy.md:21:The Best value is one saved move count selected by current size. It does not present a list of people or a comparison across many records. `session.preferences.bestMoves[String(game.size)]` chooses the value before it reaches GameStatus. A single local best is not an implemented ordered-results screen.
docs/tables-policy.md:27:Board renders a named region containing an inner `div.board` and native card buttons. CSS Grid places those buttons in row order. Each card has its own coordinate and state in its accessible name, and keyboard movement follows a dedicated spatial model.
docs/tables-policy.md:31:There is also no ARIA grid role on the current board. Its native buttons and movement handler implement the specified interaction without declaring a separate composite widget role. Board keyboard navigation traces that behavior. A review of result markup should not accidentally rename or restructure the card controls because the word “row” appears in both contexts.
docs/tables-policy.md:35:An ordered collection needs context. A visible title elsewhere on a page can be helpful, but a table caption directly identifies the collection associated with the table. That matters when reading the table independently or navigating between document structures.
docs/tables-policy.md:37:The policy does not specify caption text. The source has no existing results title. A feature's actual subject supplies the wording. Describing that subject is different from attaching a technical explanation of table markup to product copy.
docs/tables-policy.md:39:A caption also does not supply missing column meaning. A reader needs to know whether a number represents moves, a position in an ordering or another property. The required header cells carry the column relationships; the caption names the whole collection. Combining all labels into a caption would leave the row values harder to interpret.
docs/tables-policy.md:41:## Column relationships and reading order
docs/tables-policy.md:47:Row headers, column order, numeric alignment and sorting controls depend on the result feature. The current game has no result implementation with those details.
docs/tables-policy.md:51:The current game already separates outer page width from board overflow. Result data would have its own content and width behavior; it cannot simply inherit the card board's scroll logic by sharing a class name. A table's relationship between headers and values remains meaningful even when its viewport is narrow.
docs/tables-policy.md:55:Name text can also vary in width without changing its accepted value. Typography and wrapping are layout concerns. The name policies remain separate from table structure; accepted names retain their displayed representation. Typography and Form layout guide explain nearby layout decisions in the existing interface.
docs/adr-034-face-count.md:15:This structure gives the catalogue a predictable order and makes it possible to generate both drawing and name. It also means the largest board includes the full cross-product, including combinations that can be visually demanding at the minimum size. The design is not complete merely because 128 different strings can be generated.
docs/adr-034-face-count.md:31:Focused tests check all identities by shape, marker center and fill, and compare names and geometry with the ordered tables. They also test invalid IDs. Browser tests capture color, grayscale and neutralized sheets and compare rasterized silhouettes. The sheet uses the same `Card` and `Face` components as the game, preventing a parallel art renderer from concealing a component-specific issue.
