docs/adr-034-face-count.md:49:  if (!shape || !marker || !center || !color || !colorName) throw new Error('Invalid face.');
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:1:# ADR 036: Permit brief decoration under reduced motion
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:3:This historical record explains the retired duration-based exception to reduced-motion handling.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:9:After the first reduced-motion work, the team discussed whether very short decoration should count as an exception. The prototype had small state feedback rather than large page movement, and it was tempting to classify a brief effect as too minor to need a separate static path.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:11:The old decision allowed decorative animation under reduced motion when it lasted under 200 milliseconds. Duration was the proposed dividing line: short enough decoration could remain, while longer effects would be removed. This record describes that retired exception as it was understood at the time.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:15:The team wanted to preserve a feeling of responsiveness around button and card state changes. A small interpolation could make an edge or highlight feel less abrupt, and the implementation already had a short border treatment. A numerical cutoff appeared to make the distinction easy to apply during review.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:17:It also seemed to avoid a proliferation of motion branches. If a brief effect could be left alone, the author would not need an alternate declaration for every small transition. In a limited prototype, that looked like a reasonable compromise between visual polish and the broader reduced-motion intent.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:21:Removing the exception entirely would have made the reduced path more consistent but would have discarded the subtle transitions the team was trying to retain. A user-facing motion setting would have added another control and stored preference. Evaluating each effect only through subjective review would have been less easy to summarize than a duration cutoff.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:29:It also created a surprising result for users who had already expressed a reduced-motion preference in the browser or operating system. The application was interpreting “short” as sufficient reason to retain decoration, rather than examining the effect in the context of that preference.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:33:The present `card.css` removes its border transition under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, and `status.css` removes the completion animation under the same preference. The static card states and completion text remain. Those are current source observations; they do not follow from the retired under-200ms exception.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:37:## A current reduced-motion override
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:39:The existing card implementation does not rely on the historical short-duration exception. Its reduced-motion path removes the transition.
docs/adr-036-motion-opt-in.md:42:@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
docs/test-policy.md:9:The core has adjacent tests such as `game.test.ts`, `deck.test.ts`, `snapshot.test.ts` and `replay.test.ts`. Their names make the behavior under discussion easy to locate before reading the whole suite. A feature's rules.ts has a rules.test.ts beside it. Complete interaction journeys remain at the application test level, where focus, storage, browser lifecycle and delivery can be observed together.
docs/test-policy.md:11:Adjacency is useful because a transition and its evidence can be read in one pass. It does not mean every production file in the repository has a one-to-one test file. Some concerns have several focused files: completion, move limits, snapshot selection and replay ordering each get separate attention. Other helpers are exercised through a broader behavior or browser journey.
docs/test-policy.md:41:Snapshot tests check outer keys, numeric bounds, matched-position consistency and each selection form. `snapshot-selection.test.ts` focuses on nested selection invariants. A selected index must name an available position, a mismatch must involve different faces and complete matched pairs must be represented together. The decoder reconstructs its deck from the code before checking these relationships.
docs/test-policy.md:43:Replay tests add ordering and action count constraints. Equal timestamps preserve array order, while logical time advances before each activation. `replay-order.test.ts` checks a same-time third activation blocked by the preceding mismatch. These are rules about accepted actions, not browser task order.
docs/test-policy.md:57:The browser wrapper in `tests/browser-test.ts` creates a new context per journey and registers its closure with the Node test context. It collects page errors and asserts that none occurred after a successful journey body. It creates an artifact directory from the test name and restricts output paths to a filename. A shared browser and local server are closed in the file-level teardown.
docs/test-policy.md:65:Disk delivery demonstrates that the HTML carries its own code and assets. HTTP-origin journeys establish preference persistence in a browser environment with a stable storage origin for the journey. A result from one mode does not establish the other, because storage behavior on local files varies.
docs/test-policy.md:87:Source audit notes records the package, compiler, source and naming patterns inspected by the static checks. Browser setup separately writes its observed runtime versions and can enforce the named combination. These records describe different evidence; neither replaces a behavior test.
docs/test-policy.md:89:For a review, the useful account names the changed behavior and the check that observes it. Code review guide gives examples of that mapping.
docs/adr-038-no-analytics.md:19:A deterministic deal turns a vague report into a concrete starting point. A replay can isolate the exact ordering of a mismatch timeout and another activation without asking a remote logging service what happened. The pure core makes this route practical because it can execute outside the browser with the same rules.
docs/adr-038-no-analytics.md:21:This is especially useful for a game that can be opened from disk. A report need not come from a stable hosted origin, and the developer does not need access to a user's local storage. The person reporting the issue can choose what to export and share. Sharing FAQ explains which format answers which question.
docs/adr-038-no-analytics.md:25:A small anonymous event counter would still be a runtime request and a service dependency. Detailed session traces could make some investigations easier but would require a policy for collection and retention that the current product does not need. An opt-in diagnostics upload would be another feature with its own UI and server path, rather than a free addition to an existing local tool.
docs/adr-038-no-analytics.md:33:A grep for a vendor name alone would not establish the absence of networking. A generic request helper or an injected build helper could make requests without using an analytics label. Inspecting source boundaries and the actual artifact gives a more useful combination of evidence.
docs/time-policy.md:21:An unfinished imported snapshot is paused by `replaceGame`. Its saved elapsed time and mismatch remainder remain game data, while the session prevents further active advancement until Resume. Resume resets the observation baseline and clears pause-related notices. It does not restart the imported game or give a pending mismatch a new full interval.
docs/time-policy.md:27:`ActiveClock` stores `observedAt`. `createClock(now)` creates that record. `elapsedStep(clock, now, active)` computes `delta = now - clock.observedAt`; it returns the delta only when active, finite and positive. Otherwise it returns zero. In both cases it returns a new clock whose baseline is `now`.
docs/time-policy.md:33:A backward observation or a nonfinite delta produces no advancement. This is defensive handling of supplied values, not a claim that such a sequence is a normal browser clock behavior. The clock still records the supplied observation, so later arithmetic is based on that new baseline. Clock design discusses this small function and the hook separately.
docs/time-policy.md:37:`CLOCK_INTERVAL_MS` is 40. The hook uses a timeout that reschedules itself. For a mismatch it asks for the smaller of 40 and `remainingMs`; otherwise it uses 40. A scheduled delay is not added directly to the game. When the callback runs, the hook obtains a new observation and computes the actual delta.
docs/time-policy.md:45:The controller calls `clock.synchronize()` before `activateSession`. That ordering can close an overdue mismatch before the new activation is evaluated. A busy browser can delay the scheduled callback, but it does not make a logically expired interval block the next click indefinitely.
docs/time-policy.md:55:A positive remainder keeps the mismatch with its new remainder. A zero or negative remainder closes the selection. The function does not carry a queue of delayed closing events, and it does not truncate elapsed advancement to the mismatch remainder. A 900ms advance against an 800ms mismatch closes it and adds 900ms of active elapsed time.
docs/time-policy.md:57:The mismatch remainder and elapsed total serve different purposes. The total can reach its cap while a new mismatch still has time to resolve. `advanceGame` continues processing the selection even after the total is capped. Similarly, the move cap does not stop matching, time or victory. These limits protect stored/displayed numbers rather than ending play.
docs/adr-042-error-copy.md:13:`useGame.loadBoard` trims the submitted code, checks it and returns `false` after publishing the invalid-code notice if parsing fails. Only a valid code reaches clock reset and game replacement. `loadSnapshot` follows the same order with `decodeSnapshot`. This order is the behavioral foundation underneath the copy.
docs/adr-042-error-copy.md:21:The replay CLI is different. It is a developer command processing a file, so its problem map reports a more specific reason and can name a one-based action position. That is a different audience and output channel, not an inconsistency in how the browser button behaves. ADR 015 explains that command contract.
docs/adr-044-no-routing.md:27:A query parameter for the initial board could be a separate feature, but it is not implemented in the game. The documented control is the way to load a code in the current game.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:4:The game has three exchange formats with deliberately different jobs. A board code identifies a starting deal. A snapshot describes a position on that deal. A replay describes activations on a logical timeline and produces a position when evaluated. Choosing the smallest useful format keeps a report understandable: a wrong shuffle needs a code, a wrong revealed state needs a snapshot, and a timeout ordering problem usually needs a replay.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:8:## Existing formats and new exports
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:10:The current snapshot and replay formats are JSON objects with numeric `version: 1`. The board code is a string whose prefix is `np1`. Their definitions predate the general policy for new JSON exports. Every new exported JSON object has a "format" of "neon-pairs/KIND", naming what it holds, and a whole-number "version", with its keys in alphabetical order at every level. Formats that already exist stay as they are. In particular, adding a discriminator to a version 1 snapshot would make it fail the existing exact-key check, and changing the board-code string into JSON would remove its usefulness in the Board code field.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:12:There is an important distinction between naming a new exchange format and modifying a known one. `snapshot.ts` is still the authority for the fields of an exported snapshot. `replay.ts` is still the authority for a replay input. The general policy does not silently widen either accepted object. See Format versioning for the compatibility decisions around those boundaries.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:14:Current snapshot output is readable JSON produced by `JSON.stringify` with a two-space indentation argument. It writes fields in the construction order used by `encodeSnapshot`: `version`, `boardCode`, `matched`, `selection`, `moves`, `elapsedMs`, `started`. This order is not alphabetical. An importer accepts any object-key order when the required keys and values are present. The distinction matters when comparing documents: a change in whitespace or object-key order need not change the represented state.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:24:| Did a local best survive reload? | A storage-focused reproduction | An exported snapshot carries no best record |
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:26:A board code is short enough to copy into a report without a separate file. Its meaning is stable because the prefix fixes generation. A snapshot contains enough progress to avoid playing back a long game. A replay is the most explicit description of action order, but the browser does not currently record or load replay files through its reproduction panel. Developer tooling evaluates them through the core and CLI.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:32:A board code carries generation version, supported side length and seed. `generateBoard` derives the immutable deck. The result contains face identifiers in position order; the code does not carry artwork, card names or a language setting. Loading it creates a fresh hidden state with no moves and no elapsed time. The algorithm and literal example are documented in Board code format.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:36:A replay carries `boardCode`, an ordered array of `{atMs, index}` actions and `endMs`, alongside its version. It starts from the hidden deal rather than from an imported snapshot. The action timeline advances game time before each activation. The final time is advanced even when no action occurs there. This is how a replay can describe a mismatch closing after the final click. Replay format covers the timeline and diagnostic result.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:38:None of these objects contains a browser storage key, a saved size preference, a per-size best record, a focus location or an animation flag. Those values have different lifetimes and authorities. A loaded board's size becomes the chosen size through session replacement; that is a browser consequence of loading, not a separate field in the artifact. A completed import is displayed without recording an import as a new played result.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:48:Successful load resets the board's remembered tab stop to its first position through a new session generation. Load and export buttons retain focus, with Resume handling its own disappearing-button focus transition. Focus is not serialized, so sharing a position cannot restore where the sender last tabbed. See Reproduction panel and Focus policy for interface details.
docs/data-exchange-policy.md:52:The pure format functions operate on strings and game data. `parseBoardCode` returns size and seed or `null`; `decodeSnapshot` returns a reconstructed state or `null`; `readReplay` returns a final state or a classified problem. They do not operate browser controls, write storage or read files.
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:7:Neon Pairs is an interactive memory task. The player reveals information over time, and the game blocks new activation during a mismatch. A printed page cannot reproduce that interaction. The design discussion therefore did not treat a paper board as an alternate delivery mode.
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:9:The decision was to omit dedicated print styles. The current CSS contains screen layout, focus, hover and reduced-motion behavior, but no print-specific game rendering. This does not prevent a browser from offering Print; it means the project does not provide a separately designed paper experience.
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:13:Printing a closed board would show identical backs and little useful state. Printing every face would reveal the deal rather than reproduce play. Printing current progress might help a report, but a static page would lose the remaining active interval and the meaning of a first or second reveal. Choosing among those outputs would require a new feature definition.
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:39:This is a screen-oriented scroll container. There is no alternate paper layout in this rule set; a print feature would need to define its own intended information and geometry.
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:47:  --board-border: 1px;
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:52:  border: var(--board-border) solid var(--border);
docs/adr-046-print-styles.md:53:  border-radius: 12px;
docs/dialog-policy.md:5:The current source contains no dialog component, native dialog element or leave-work handler. New Game, Restart, board-code loading and snapshot loading call the controller directly.
docs/dialog-policy.md:9:A confirmation is useful only when it corresponds to a real decision about abandoning work. Its content and focus need to make the conservative path understandable before the player commits to leaving. Leaving unfinished work asks first in a native dialog element with two buttons, Keep playing and Leave. Keep playing takes focus when it opens, and Escape keeps playing. The decision is about the unfinished work being left; opening a disclosure to inspect a code is not itself that decision.
docs/dialog-policy.md:11:There is no third action specified by this policy. In particular, the existing snapshot exporter does not turn this confirmation into an automatic save workflow. Export is a separate user action with a text result. A feature that uses the confirmation can draw on that distinction without presenting an unsaved draft as if it had already been preserved.
docs/dialog-policy.md:13:The policy does not define which future feature owns a leave route or a new navigation screen. Those boundaries have to be visible in the feature's actual code. The current page has no router and no alternate play screen. Focus policy covers orientation when a feature introduces a main-area replacement.
docs/dialog-policy.md:17:`Controls.tsx` renders New Game and Restart as native buttons. Their callbacks in `use-game.ts` reset the clock adapter and commit a replacement game. They do not consult `started`, Moves, Pairs or a dialog result before doing so. The existing game replaces immediately when those buttons are used.
docs/dialog-policy.md:21:The native `details` panel is also not a modal. It expands in document flow and leaves the rest of the page operable. Calling its summary a dialog trigger would misdescribe both the DOM and its focus behavior. Reproduction panel documents the actual disclosure.
docs/dialog-policy.md:23:This distinction matters during incremental work. A new dialog in one feature does not silently change the semantics of every old control. When reading a change, follow the event handler to see whether it reaches a confirmation boundary, a parser, or the replacement directly. Similar visual labels do not establish the same lifecycle.
docs/dialog-policy.md:27:The policy names a native element because the interface already relies on browser semantics for buttons, forms, selects and details. A dialog has a different interaction boundary from an ordinary panel. Its opening, cancellation and closing need to be considered as a coherent interaction rather than a div painted over the board.
docs/dialog-policy.md:31:The distinction between cancellation and commitment is observable in game state. Continuing leaves the same deal, revealed selection and counters available. A committed departure may dispose of or replace the session, depending on the feature. The current game has no dialog-specific storage write or serialized marker.
docs/dialog-policy.md:33:No dialog state exists in `Session`. Its fields are `game`, `paused`, `generation`, `feedback`, `loadNotice`, `announcement`, `celebration` and `preferences`. The existing `paused` flag specifically represents unfinished snapshot import. Reusing that flag for a new overlay without considering its other consumers would also affect Resume and load notices.
docs/dialog-policy.md:37:A mismatch stores its remaining active milliseconds in game selection. The session clock advances it when the page is visible and the imported-pause flag is clear. Merely placing a dialog above the board is not one of those conditions in the current source.
docs/dialog-policy.md:39:Consequently, there is no existing rule here that opening a confirmation pauses the elapsed clock or mismatch. A feature implementation needs to be read for its actual timing behavior rather than assuming that every modal surface freezes play. Clock design and Mismatch interval explain the established clock model.
docs/dialog-policy.md:41:Replacement does have a well-defined lifecycle today. `clock.reset()` and the session generation prevent old pending updates from affecting a replacement game. If a leave decision leads to that existing path, stale-timer protection still belongs to the session. The dialog does not need to know the two selected card indices to implement that protection.
docs/dialog-policy.md:43:The period between opening and making a decision is therefore worth observing in review. It can expose accidental differences between the visual state, current game state and timers. The observation is useful even when the feature's intended behavior is simply to preserve the current clock semantics.
docs/dialog-policy.md:47:Opening the confirmation establishes a temporary interaction context. The policy above states its initial focus and cancellation result. That avoids relying on DOM order alone to make the safe continuation choice the first keyboard target.
docs/dialog-policy.md:49:After a decision, the durability of the source control matters. Existing action buttons generally remain mounted after a deal replacement, while Resume removes itself. Game handles Resume by moving focus before the button is removed. That is a concrete example of a disappearing source, though it is not a dialog implementation.
docs/dialog-policy.md:51:A leave feature may keep or remove its trigger as part of its surrounding screen. Its focus return depends on which controls remain mounted. A review can inspect the final active element and its visibility.
docs/dialog-policy.md:61:Current browser tests establish focus retention on ordinary buttons, invalid draft preservation, Resume's transfer order and stale-timer protection. They do not establish a dialog journey. Any future report of dialog coverage has to point at the corresponding implementation and evidence, rather than citing those tests because they also involve focus.
docs/focus-policy.md:3:This page records focus behavior in the current game and the policy for a feature that changes the main screen.
docs/focus-policy.md:9:`Game.tsx` currently renders one main area with an h1, deal controls, statistics, instructions, turn output, the Board and reproduction controls. It has no alternate-screen branch or screen-level h2 focus target. Replacing a deal remounts the board within that page; it does not replace the page with a different screen.
docs/focus-policy.md:11:A feature that introduces a screen replacement has a different orientation problem from resetting a deck. When a feature replaces what the main area shows, focus moves to that screen's h2, which has tabindex="-1". This gives the new screen a programmatic landing place without adding that heading to ordinary Tab traversal. The current deal and load callbacks can still be understood on their own terms; they are not evidence that such a screen transition already exists.
docs/focus-policy.md:13:The policy applies to the feature-level replacement described here. Expanding native details, updating statistics, closing a mismatch or displaying the completed-game paragraph does not currently create a new screen. Those operations retain the page structure and follow the existing behavior below. Dialog policy covers a separate interaction with its own opening focus.
docs/focus-policy.md:37:Arrow movement uses the DOM order of the deck and never changes game selection on its own. Tab and Shift+Tab are left to the browser, so the board is one stop in the page sequence rather than a trap. Returning to it finds the last focused card, even if that card is now matched.
docs/focus-policy.md:47:The ordering is observable. `tests/control-contract.e2e.test.ts` installs a focus listener and confirms that Resume is still present when the card receives focus. A visual assertion after the fact would not establish this timing. The focus-entry test also remembers a far card on a large imported board, scrolls away and confirms that Resume returns to that location with nearest alignment.
docs/focus-policy.md:63:Winning does not move focus to the completion paragraph in GameStatus. The final card stays the current control, and the completed summary becomes available in document order. The final turn's feedback is published through the game's event output. The component does not force the player to leave the board simply because all pairs are now matched.
docs/focus-policy.md:73:A useful focus report names the source control, action, destination and whether the source remains mounted. For example: “Enter in Board code loads 6x6, replaces the input and leaves Load board focused.” That is more precise than “load focuses correctly,” and it exposes why failure has a different result. Interface review checklist gathers these journeys with layout and input checks.
docs/focus-policy.md:75:For new screen work, the source review begins by locating the actual replacement boundary. For changes within the existing game page, it begins with the current action-specific behavior above. Screen replacement and the established board lifecycle have separate focus behavior.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:7:Replay actions are processed in their array order, with logical time advanced to each action's `atMs` before activating its card. Times must be nondecreasing. Equal timestamps are valid and do not trigger sorting or deduplication. After the final action, time advances to `endMs`.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:9:This defines the boundary between a mismatch closing and a new reveal. If a mismatch expires exactly at an action time, the close happens first. The action then sees a closed selection and can reveal a card. Reversing the order would cause that activation to be ignored as if it had arrived during the mismatch.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:13:`readReplay` starts from `createGame(boardCode)` with `previousTime = 0`. For each validated action, it computes the difference from the previous timestamp, calls `advanceGame` and passes the resulting state to `activateCard`. The nesting in the source makes the order visible in one expression.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:15:A zero delta leaves time unchanged but does not suppress the action. Two equal-time indices can reveal a matching pair in a single timestamp. Three equal-time indices still execute sequentially; the third observes whatever selection the first two produced. If that is a mismatch, the normal ignored-activation rule applies.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:19:Replay timestamps are logical positions in a trace. The game's elapsed clock starts at its first valid reveal, so waiting before that action does not become elapsed play. After completion, later timeline advancement does not extend the completed game's time. The replay can therefore have a large `endMs` without implying equal elapsed milliseconds in the final snapshot.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:21:Times use nonnegative safe integers. The game's elapsed value has its separate cap of 359999999ms. The parser does not shorten the accepted timestamp range to that cap. This distinction is important when diagnosing a replay with a long pre-start interval or long post-completion tail. Replay format explains the fields.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:25:The parser rejects timestamps that go backward or exceed `endMs`, malformed action objects and out-of-range indices. An in-range activation can still be ignored under ordinary game rules; that is valid replay behavior rather than malformed input. Validation establishes a valid instruction sequence, not that every instruction changes state.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:31:Sorting actions by time would conceal invalid order and could change equal-time semantics. Resolving time after activation would create an off-by-one event ordering at mismatch boundaries. Treating simultaneous actions as an unordered set would leave first and second reveal undefined. The selected loop avoids all three ambiguities.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:33:Focused replay tests cover boundary times, equal-time order, ignored actions, caps and final advancement. Independent examples let another implementation verify the same semantics without a browser. Browser clock-edge tests then check that its adapter synchronizes before real activation as well. ADR 013, ADR 021 and Turn resolution explain those complementary contracts.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:37:The action loop preserves the submitted order. Advance-before-activate is explicit, and final advancement uses the same rule function.
docs/adr-048-replay-order.md:51:      return { problem: ReplayProblem.order, action: position };
docs/import-safety-policy.md:10:The implementation already rejects extra keys, but does not yet produce that field-specific notice. `hasExactKeys` returns a boolean, `decodeSnapshot` returns `null`, and the browser displays `Invalid snapshot.` for all snapshot failures. `readReplay` provides problem categories rather than the offending key name. The current adapters reject extensions without identifying the offending field as the diagnostic policy requires.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:12:The existing code does not define a traversal order, object-path syntax or combined-error format for a field-specific diagnostic. Its boolean exact-key helper cannot report a first key. Those details cannot be inferred from a `null` result. For current behavior, examine the published notice and replay problem category rather than expecting a key name.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:40:Exact keys are checked as a set, not as an ordering constraint. Missing fields fail as well as extra fields. Nested objects are checked at their own format boundaries. A `closed` selection with an `index` is invalid even if that index would be an eligible position in a different selection variant. A replay action with an annotation field is invalid even if `atMs` and `index` are otherwise good.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:42:The helpers operate on the parsed object, not the original token stream. JSON duplicate property names are consequently handled by `JSON.parse` before exact-key checking; the decoder does not retain a record of earlier overwritten occurrences. Likewise, numeric notation such as `1.0` becomes the number `1`. The integer predicate checks the value it receives rather than enforcing a preferred spelling of a JSON number.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:62:`readReplay` checks root shape, version, board code, action count and `endMs` before walking actions. Each action then needs its exact object shape, safe nonnegative numbers, a nondecreasing timestamp no later than `endMs`, and a position inside the deck. Its index into the action array is included in action-specific failures.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:68:Action timestamps may be much larger than the elapsed clock cap. The replay evaluator advances the logical timeline using safe integers, while `advanceGame` caps the resulting elapsed counter. That distinction prevents a legitimate long timeline from being rejected merely because the visible clock has saturated. Replay format explains exact timeout edges and equal-time order.
docs/import-safety-policy.md:74:Replay reports have more detail. `readReplay` distinguishes file size, JSON syntax, root format, board code, actions array, end time, individual action shape, time order and index bounds. The CLI translates these into stderr text and uses a one-based action number for readers. Internal action positions remain zero-based. Mixing these conventions can lead an engineer to inspect the wrong action.
docs/adr-050-completion-banner.md:9:A played win receives one 600ms pulse, then the banner stays static. Under reduced motion it is static immediately. A completed snapshot also has a static completed presentation. The summary's informational role is independent of whether a celebration effect is selected.
docs/adr-050-completion-banner.md:21:An overlay could make the result prominent but would cover information and create dialog focus responsibilities. A transient toast would disappear while the completed state remained. The in-place paragraph keeps the result available without a dismissal task. ADR 019 and ADR 031 preserve related retired ideas rather than governing this banner.
docs/adr-050-completion-banner.md:27:The banner pulse changes background and opacity briefly, with one iteration. It does not shower the board with moving objects or repeatedly request attention. Its reduced-motion override removes the effect while keeping the same text and border. ADR 025 explains that presentation path.
docs/adr-050-completion-banner.md:31:A blocking result dialog would have created an extra step before another game. Replacing the entire board with a score card would have discarded useful visual context. Doing nothing except leaving all faces up would have made the endpoint less explicit and harder to discover through a status-oriented journey.
docs/motion-policy.md:2:This page describes the motion currently used by Neon Pairs, its reduced-motion behavior, and the source and tests behind it.
docs/motion-policy.md:4:The game has two authored motion effects: a card border transition and a completion pulse. Neither moves a card, changes its dimensions, or delays a game-state update. The relevant declarations live with their components in `src/game/board/card.css` and `src/game/status/status.css`. The broader visual specification is Visuals; turn timing belongs in Turn resolution.
docs/motion-policy.md:6:## Card borders
docs/motion-policy.md:8:The `.card` rule declares `transition: border-color 120ms ease`. On hover, `.card:hover` changes the border to `var(--text)`. A matched card instead uses `var(--green)`. The transition is limited to the border color: background, outline, size, opacity and SVG geometry have no transition in this rule.
docs/motion-policy.md:10:This distinction matters when reading a recording. A reveal replaces the back with the face and changes the card background immediately. The border can still be between colors if the pointer has just entered or left the card. A screenshot taken during that interval does not show the final border value. In `tests/art.e2e.test.ts`, the comparison helper waits for the card's animations to finish before collecting computed styles for this reason.
docs/motion-policy.md:14:Hover also has no gameplay effect. `tests/style.e2e.test.ts` records all computed properties before and after hover, checks that only border-color properties differ, and compares exported game progress. That test is useful when an apparently small visual edit adds a shadow, scale transform or other hover change. Such a difference would depart from the existing V16 behavior even if the card still looked recognizable.
docs/motion-policy.md:16:## Reduced motion
docs/motion-policy.md:18:Both component stylesheets contain a `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` media query. The card query sets `transition: none`; the status query sets `animation: none` on `.celebrate`. Under prefers-reduced-motion: reduce, new elements run no animation and no transition. This keeps the static visual states available immediately when the preference is active. The existing cards still change their border and artwork when their state changes; the change simply has no interpolated border effect.
docs/motion-policy.md:22:A useful review includes setting the preference before the page loads and completing a board in that mode. `tests/motion.e2e.test.ts` does this through browser media emulation, then checks the banner's computed animation name and a card's transition duration. Those assertions check the existing effects directly. They do not infer reduced-motion support from a screenshot that happens to have been taken after an animation finished.
docs/motion-policy.md:30:The keyframes specify a midpoint only. At 50%, the paragraph background is `var(--panel)` and opacity is `0.75`; the start and end come from its underlying styles. There is no `animation-fill-mode` that preserves the midpoint appearance. After the one iteration, the banner remains present in its normal static styling. Its border, padding and text are not removed when the animation finishes.
docs/motion-policy.md:32:This is a pulse of the paragraph as a whole, so the opacity also affects its text and border during the effect. It is not a highlight painted behind otherwise opaque text. Contrast audit separates opaque token ratios from a composited estimate for this midpoint. That distinction is helpful when evaluating a captured frame halfway through completion.
docs/motion-policy.md:34:The effect neither loops nor emits particles. V18 describes one pulse, a static reduced-motion result, and the absence of flashing and confetti. There are no pulse keyframes on individual cards. A finished board continues to show its matched artwork at full card opacity, as specified in V15.
docs/motion-policy.md:38:The boolean is session data, not a CSS guess based on how many green borders appear. `initialSession` initializes `celebration` to false. `activateSession` sets it from the result of `isWon(game)` after an effective activation that resolves a turn. `replaceGame` clears it when the session changes deals or loads a snapshot.
docs/motion-policy.md:42:The flag can remain true after the CSS animation has finished. That does not make the effect repeat on every timer update: an existing element retaining the same animation class is different from mounting a new element or changing its animation name. The final game also stops advancing its active elapsed time. The relevant session behavior is covered more fully in Session module.
docs/motion-policy.md:46:The board's scrolling code uses nearest alignment. `Board.tsx` calls `scrollIntoView` after a click, and the focus hook uses the same alignment for keyboard focus. Neither call specifies smooth behavior. The source CSS has no `scroll-behavior: smooth` declaration. Ordinary touch inertia belongs to the browser and is not the authored completion pulse or border transition.
docs/motion-policy.md:50:The pointer path also deliberately postpones its own scroll adjustment until the click handler runs. Moving a partly clipped card during pointer focus could shift the release target and lose the click. A motion review that only activates fully visible cards misses this interaction. The existing pointer tests include clipped-edge presses and touch taps at the phone, desktop and equivalent zoom layouts.
docs/motion-policy.md:54:The normal-motion test checks a `0.12` second card transition whose property list contains only `border-color`. After finishing a board, it checks a `0.6` second banner animation with one iteration. The reduced-motion test checks `animationName === 'none'` on the banner and a parsed card transition duration of zero. These are source-level and computed-style checks.
docs/motion-policy.md:58:No motion duration here controls whether two cards match or when a mismatch closes. Those decisions come from the game rules and active time. The presentation stays synchronized by rendering their result, rather than waiting for a CSS event to authorize the next action. That separation is the main maintenance benefit of the small current motion surface.
docs/adr-052-release-tags.md:9:The release discussion favored tags as named source checkpoints: a way to associate a delivered game and its evidence with the source that produced them.
docs/adr-052-release-tags.md:25:Relying only on the latest branch would make older bug reports hard to associate with the source used at the time. Naming a downloaded HTML file manually could help a person but would not identify a repository checkpoint. Embedding a release service in the browser would be a much larger feature and would conflict with the independent offline artifact.
docs/adr-052-release-tags.md:33:The README records successful checks on the named combination. Release checklist, ADR 029 and ADR 055 explain the neighboring review concerns.
docs/adr-052-release-tags.md:43:  "name": "neon-pairs-reference",
docs/randomness-policy.md:5:A new board needs an unpredictable seed. A board code, restart, imported snapshot and replay need the opposite property: a particular code always reconstructs the same deck. The implementation keeps those two tasks separate rather than letting a shuffle consult ambient state. Deck generation covers the deal algorithm as a format; this page concentrates on its source ownership and integration.
docs/randomness-policy.md:9:`src/game/session/fresh-board.ts` exports `freshBoardCode(size, previousSeed?)`. It obtains a seed and returns the normal board-code representation through `boardCode` from `deck.ts`. Only src/game/session/fresh-board.ts may call a random source. Everything else that needs randomness imports it from there. The session controller uses that exported operation when initializing a session or asking for a new game.
docs/randomness-policy.md:25:When `previousSeed` is supplied, a candidate equal to it is discarded. `use-game.ts` passes the current game's seed when creating a new game, including when a different grid size is selected. This means the next seed differs from the immediately preceding seed even across a size change.
docs/randomness-policy.md:29:The retry loop also does not claim a bounded number of calls. It retries out-of-range draws and an excluded candidate. There is no time-based fallback in this module. A browser crypto failure is not caught here. The storage module's tolerant behavior should not be mistaken for a universal fallback around every browser API.
docs/randomness-policy.md:41:`deck.ts` exports `nextRandomState(state)`, but this function is arithmetic over an explicit argument. It computes `(state * 16_807) % 2_147_483_647`. Repeating a call with the same state returns the same result. The name describes a pseudorandom sequence; it does not imply a browser read.
docs/randomness-policy.md:76:This table is useful when a bug report says that “a new board changed.” A new game is expected to change the seed. A restart or imported code is expected to repeat the existing deal. A size selection is a fresh deal rather than a geometric rearrangement of the old cards.
docs/randomness-policy.md:88:The source audit's core list rejects browser entropy references in the named core files. It does not implement a whole-program information-flow proof or a general scanner for every possible source of variation. Source audit notes describes precisely what it scans.
docs/adr-054-acceptance-journeys.md:7:A game can pass isolated assertions and still fail at the boundary between them. A new deal can look correct until an old mismatch callback closes a newly revealed card. A loaded snapshot can look correct until its Resume button disappears with focus still on it. Acceptance needs sequences that cross those boundaries.
docs/adr-054-acceptance-journeys.md:13:The play journey completes every supported size and checks move accounting, matches, misses and best records. The interruption journey starts or restarts during a mismatch and waits beyond the old interval. The keyboard journey enters the board, learns names, resolves turns, completes, leaves with Tab and starts again.
docs/adr-054-acceptance-journeys.md:25:A button can have a correct name in isolation while the whole game is difficult to traverse. The complete 4x4 keyboard journey checks controls, positional names, revealed faces, turn feedback, final summary and exit. Browser accessibility-tree evidence makes those observations reviewable.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:3:This page describes how unavailable browser storage affects play and how saved-record features present that limitation.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:5:The current game isolates storage failures from the active game. It catches errors while obtaining the browser store, reading the preference key and writing its replacement. The session can hold its selected size and best results in memory even when no durable write succeeds. There is no current preference-storage warning in the rendered game.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:9:For a feature that presents saved records, the unavailable-storage state belongs near the data it affects. The active interaction can still use its current in-memory state even though later visits cannot rely on finding that state again. When browser storage is blocked, a feature keeps working in memory and shows "Progress won't be saved on this device." where its saved records would appear. This ties the explanation to the missing persistence rather than turning it into a failure of the whole game.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:11:The preference adapter does not yet expose a blocked-status result. `readPreferences` returns a `Preferences` value in all cases, and `writePreferences` returns `void`. Neither return type carries an availability flag or failure reason. `Game.tsx` renders no preference-storage warning; saved-record features have separate failure feedback.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:17:The first operation is acquiring the storage object. `browserStore` in `src/game/session/use-saved-preferences.ts` returns `window.localStorage` inside a `try` block. If the property getter throws, the function catches the exception and returns `null`. A denied property getter is tested in `tests/browser-environment.e2e.test.ts` by defining a getter that throws a `SecurityError` before the game initializes.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:23:These stages explain why a single boolean based on the existence of the storage property would be a weak diagnostic. The game doesn't use such a probe. It attempts the real operation at the boundary and catches failure there.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:31:If storage remains unavailable, a page reload starts another session from the fallback. The earlier in-memory best is gone with the old page. This is the expected consequence of unsuccessful saving, even though play in the earlier page was complete and valid. A report that a score survived Restart but disappeared on reload is consistent with a write failure.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:45:There is no queue, timer-driven retry, backoff or unload handler. Another dependency change can cause another attempt, but the code does not poll for a browser setting to change. A returned `null` store is captured in state for the lifetime of that mounted game; enabling storage later does not replace that state value automatically.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:47:If the captured store object exists and its methods change behavior, a later effect can succeed using the same object. For example, a test double can reject its first write and accept a later one. That is different from reacquiring a previously inaccessible property. Both cases can be modeled without making the game rules aware of browser error names.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:55:Similarly, a quota error does not mean the existing value is invalid. The old string may remain perfectly readable while the new result exists only in memory. A person may see a better current best and then see the older best after reload. That sequence is a useful way to reproduce a failed update without damaging a valid baseline.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:61:The source only calls `getItem` and `setItem` for `PREFERENCES_KEY`. It has no storage-wide clear call, key enumeration or cleanup routine. `PreferenceStore` exposes only those two methods. The storage unit test explicitly seeds an unrelated key and checks that a preference write leaves its value intact.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:69:`storage.test.ts` uses a denied store whose read and write methods both throw. It checks that reading yields the default size and that writing does not throw. It also covers `readPreferences(null)`. This verifies the API boundary directly, without needing to configure an actual browser's privacy settings.
docs/storage-failure-policy.md:73:These tests don't establish every browser policy or a physical disk limit. They also don't assert the saved-record feature notice described earlier. Reading the test title alone can obscure those limits; the actual injection point and assertions show what each test proves. Storage testing guide extends that evidence into an investigation workflow.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:3:This record explains why the playable game has no feature-flag system and how test switches remain separate.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:7:The current game has one supported product behavior for each documented action. It does not choose between old and new turn rules through runtime feature flags. There is no flag client, remote configuration request or player-facing experimental-mode control in the application.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:17:## Branches that are not feature flags
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:19:The application naturally branches on state. A mismatch blocks activation; an imported game may be paused; a completed board shows a banner; reduced-motion preference changes effects. These branches express documented session and browser behavior. They are not alternate product experiments controlled by a rollout service.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:33:The cost is that the game does not support selectively enabling unfinished features for different players. That capability is outside the current product and would require its own design if ever justified.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:35:ADR 016, ADR 027, Source audit notes and ADR 052 explain the neighboring choices. The useful outcome is a reportable behavior path whose important variations are already named in game state, browser preferences and versioned data.
docs/adr-056-no-feature-flags.md:55:  assert.deepEqual([...new Set(reads.map((read) => read.replaceAll('\\', '/')))].sort(), runners);
docs/target-size-policy.md:9:The existing code already gives cards a minimum track size and shared controls a minimum height. Extending the interface calls for checking the actual rendered target in the phone layout, where flex wrapping and available width matter. Every new control is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels at a 390 pixel wide viewport. This policy covers the rendered control area rather than enlarging only a decorative child or a focus ring.
docs/target-size-policy.md:11:The current styles are useful building blocks, but they are not proof of every dimension for an arbitrary new element. In particular, `.control` declares minimum height without a general minimum width. A new short-label action could be tall enough while still being too narrow. The policy check and the existing class definition therefore answer related but different questions.
docs/target-size-policy.md:25:`game.css` applies `box-sizing: border-box` globally. The declared height constraints therefore include padding and border. Looking only at text line height would underestimate the target, while adding padding to the measured border box would overestimate it.
docs/target-size-policy.md:27:Cards have `width: 100%`, `aspect-ratio: 1`, zero padding and a `2px` border. Their width comes from the grid track. They do not establish their minimum using the SVG's coordinates or intrinsic artwork size. The current artwork can change shape while the card target remains the same square.
docs/target-size-policy.md:31:The inner board calculates a minimum content width from side length, `--card-min` and `--card-gap`. On a sixteen-column board that is 794px before viewport padding and border. A phone cannot display that whole width at once while keeping the current card minimum.
docs/target-size-policy.md:45:A visual review at a wide desktop window can miss the consequences of a new wrapper or label. Selectors such as `.controls > button` and `.control-row > button` apply to direct children. If a wrapper changes which element grows, measuring the wrapper rather than the actual button can hide a target-width problem.
docs/target-size-policy.md:47:The overall content has `16px` page padding and a maximum width of `1120px`. A viewport width is therefore not the same thing as a control's available row width. Panel padding, gaps and borders consume space too. Reading the size policy as a viewport measurement alone would skip the layout where the target actually lives.
docs/target-size-policy.md:53:The neutral back is a `12px` span with a rotated border and `aria-hidden="true"`. It is deliberately smaller than the card. Counting its bounds as the input target would describe the drawing rather than the event-handling element. Similarly, the matched check is a state mark, not a separate action with its own click listener.
docs/target-size-policy.md:55:Focus outlines sit outside the control border and do not add activation area. Hover border changes likewise do not expand the target. Measurements used to assess the policy concern the actual element box; measurements used to assess focus visibility include outline width and offset. The project already has separate helpers for that distinction in `tests/focus-bounds.ts`.
docs/target-size-policy.md:73:For a new feature review, a concise measurement record can identify the viewport, the control's role and name, its width and height and whether it is fully reachable. The record is most useful alongside the rendered layout, because it can reveal that a formally large button is covered by another element or forced beyond the page edge. This is observation of the target in context, not a second numerical policy.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:1:# ADR 058: Give additional stored data a distinct namespace
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:3:This record explains the naming rule for new stored data while preserving the existing preferences key.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:7:The first storage decision used a game prefix, a data-family segment and a version suffix separated by dots. The current preferences key still demonstrates that choice: `neon-pairs.preferences.v1`. It contains a selected size and a map of per-size best moves in a versioned JSON value.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:13:The namespace distinguishes newly introduced storage from keys that a released game already reads. New stored data uses a key of the form np3:THING, where THING is a lowercase plural noun for what is stored. Keys that already exist keep their names. This lets a reader recognize the new convention while the existing preference reader continues to find its current value.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:19:`PREFERENCES_KEY` is the one existing key used by `readPreferences` and `writePreferences`. The stored JSON carries `schemaVersion: 1`, `size` and `bestMoves`. Naming a storage key and validating the value are separate responsibilities. A recognizable namespace cannot establish that JSON is well formed, that a size is supported or that a score is a plausible bounded integer.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:21:The parser treats saved data as unknown, checks supported values and falls back to usable defaults when access or validation fails. Writes are caught so the in-memory game remains playable. The namespace decision does not move those browser effects into pure game functions. Preferences store and Storage failure policy describe the existing behavior.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:25:Renaming every key at once would make the storage inspector look uniform, but would create work and risk unrelated to the new naming need. Continuing the original dotted pattern indefinitely would avoid any new convention while leaving this replacement decision without effect. The selected approach separates continuity for existing data from a clear naming choice for additions.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:27:A single giant application value could avoid choosing new keys at all. That would couple unrelated data families to the same read, validation and write path. The current preferences envelope is appropriately small for size and best moves.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:31:The namespace marker is not a new board-code prefix, snapshot version or replay version. Those public formats retain their own identifiers. It is also not a claim that the current preferences schema has been upgraded. Confusing these layers would create a format change where only storage-key naming was intended.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:33:The current source provides a concrete compatibility check: a reader can inspect `PREFERENCES_KEY` and see the existing name. There is no second preferences key to merge and no new data family to initialize as part of this naming decision. Persistence envelope, Format versioning guide and ADR 053 explain the surrounding boundaries.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:35:This preserves the useful local-storage model while making the successor to the old naming decision explicit. Historical context remains in the superseded record; current readers can follow its replacement link here without treating the old pattern as the convention for additional data.
docs/adr-058-storage-namespace.md:39:These are the existing declarations. The new naming decision preserves this identifier rather than silently changing where the application reads its preferences.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:3:This page records the treatment of person-entered names and distinguishes it from the game's existing code and JSON fields.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:5:Neon Pairs currently asks for a board code and snapshot text, not a player name. There is no name field, group-name store or name rendering component in the source. The name policy below applies when such input is introduced; it does not describe a transformation already applied to the reproduction textarea.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:7:## One name representation
docs/text-entry-policy.md:9:A name can arrive with incidental spaces from typing, pasting or an input method. Keeping several representations of that same entered name would make later display and comparison harder to reason about. Every name a person types is stored and shown after trimming both ends, collapsing each run of inner whitespace to one space and normalizing to NFC. The stored value and visible value therefore describe the same processed name rather than exposing the original draft in one view and a different spelling in another.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:11:The policy is about a person's name. It does not turn all user input into name text, and it does not change the board-code grammar or snapshot serialization. The current fields demonstrate why that distinction matters: significant punctuation and structural whitespace belong to their own formats.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:13:For names, the other checks are documented separately in Name length policy and Duplicate names policy. This page explains representation and input boundaries without duplicating their conditions or error messages.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:17:The following examples use quotes only to make surrounding whitespace visible. The quotes are not part of the entered name.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:21:| `  Mira  ` | `Mira` | Space around the name is removed |
docs/text-entry-policy.md:27:The last example matters because representation cleanup is not an instruction to remove meaningful punctuation or rewrite a person's preferred spelling. The policy does not transliterate scripts, strip accents or convert names to ASCII. Those would be additional transformations beyond what is stated.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:29:NFC addresses canonical equivalence in Unicode text. It is not the compatibility normalization form. Text that merely looks similar is not necessarily canonically equivalent, and this policy does not define visual resemblance as identity. That boundary prevents a representation step from quietly becoming a broad name-matching heuristic.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:31:A name can also contain multiple words. Collapsing a whitespace run does not merge words together; it leaves a separating space. Treating the policy as “delete all whitespace” would change `Ada Lovelace` into a different string and would not implement the stated behavior.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:35:A draft is what someone is still editing. A stored value is what an action has accepted. The policy determines the representation of accepted and displayed names, but it does not prescribe that the caret jump after every keystroke or that an input method's partial composition be rewritten while it is active.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:37:That distinction leaves room to implement an editing experience without confusing it with persistence. A reviewer can inspect where a completed entry crosses from local draft state into the feature's model and where the model is rendered. If display uses an old raw draft while storage uses the processed value, the two surfaces no longer have the relationship described above.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:39:The current Reproduction component provides a useful example of draft ownership, though its data is not a name. Its snapshot string lives in local React state and is sent to the decoder on Load snapshot. BoardCodeForm instead reads its native form field at submission. Either storage approach can host an input boundary; the choice of controlled or uncontrolled field does not by itself implement name representation.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:41:No name persistence shape exists in `session/storage.ts`. Its preferences concern size and best move counts. Those fields contain no name preference key, snapshot property or import format.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:49:Successful submission replaces the game and refreshes the field from the current code because the input is keyed by generation. Unsuccessful submission preserves the draft. This gives a concrete reference for separating a draft from an accepted representation without applying name transformations to a deal identifier. See Reproduction panel.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:57:Applying name cleanup to the entire JSON draft would conflate two kinds of input. Whitespace can occur between tokens or inside a JSON string, with different meanings. The existing code passes the complete draft to `decodeSnapshot`; it does not first treat it as display prose or a person's name.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:59:The same is true for error preservation. On malformed JSON, the text remains editable and the current game remains present. That behavior supports correction of a structured draft. Accepted names use the normalized representation described above.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:67:The policy also leaves capitalization alone at this representation step. A comparison operation may have its own policy, but that does not require changing a person's displayed capitalization. Keeping display representation and comparison purpose distinct avoids presenting a comparison key as someone's chosen name.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:69:Likewise, a font's treatment of a character does not change the text stored. A missing glyph, a ligature or a different script's shaping belongs to rendering and typography. The representation policy can be implemented correctly while a layout still needs work to display the name legibly. Typography provides the visual context.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:71:The current game has no name-character allowlist or prohibited-name list. Adding one while implementing spacing cleanup would be a separate product decision, not a consequence of Unicode normalization.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:75:There are no name-specific tests in the repository because no name entry has been implemented. The useful evidence for a future implementation would connect its draft, accepted model and displayed text using the cases above. A check confined to an input's current value can miss a storage path that retains an older raw string.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:77:Repeated processing should not progressively change the accepted spelling. The transformations described here have a stable result once applied. If editing, saving and displaying the same accepted name repeatedly removes more content each time, that points to an implementation doing more than the stated representation work.
docs/text-entry-policy.md:79:The current code's strongest relevant lesson is explicit boundaries: BoardCodeForm submits a draft, the controller validates, replacement updates the model, and rendered controls follow that model. Names need their own representation boundary within the feature that introduces them. The present board-code and snapshot controls use their own input boundaries.
docs/color-policy.md:4:The palette is a dark page with pale text, neutral borders and eight bright artwork colors. The source has a shared set of CSS custom properties in `src/game/game.css` and an ordered artwork palette in `src/game/board/face-design.ts`. These two locations serve different purposes: one styles surfaces and interface states, while the other derives a face from its numeric identity. Theme tokens inventories the declarations.
docs/color-policy.md:8:The page background is `#090B14`. Panels and native form controls use `#141827`; closed cards use `#1B2034`; face-up cards use `#111625`. These dark surfaces are intentionally close in value. A reader does not have to distinguish the board's panel from the page background to find its boundary because the scroll area also has a neutral border.
docs/color-policy.md:12:`#757C9A` supplies neutral borders, and the keyboard focus outline is white. The focus outline is a separate visual layer from the card border: it can be seen while the card remains green after a match. There is no need to replace the persistent state border with a focus color. The shape of the outline and its offset also distinguish focus from a border change.
docs/color-policy.md:16:A matched card retains its face, gains a green border and displays a check mark in the upper-right corner. State is never shown by color alone, so a winner is marked with the text "(winner)" after the name. The card implementation similarly includes a non-color mark alongside its green state border. Its accessible name also carries its state, independently of the SVG appearance.
docs/color-policy.md:18:In the current game, `Card.tsx` draws the matched check as an inline SVG, and `describeCard` supplies the words `closed`, `revealed` or `matched` in the button name. The renderer does not infer those words by reading computed colors. That makes the state understandable in the DOM even when colors are unavailable or customized by the browser.
docs/color-policy.md:22:Completion appears as a sentence above the board, not as a board-wide recoloring. The text includes the result and the recorded performance. The short completion pulse described in Motion policy is supplementary: the same sentence persists after it ends and is immediately static in the reduced-motion presentation.
docs/color-policy.md:24:## The artwork palette is ordered data
docs/color-policy.md:26:The ordered colors are Cyan `#63E7FF`, Magenta `#FF71D6`, Green `#8BEA7D`, Yellow `#FFE082`, Orange `#FFB064`, Violet `#B49AFF`, Rose `#FF8296` and Ice `#D9F3FF`. These names appear in lowercase in generated face descriptions. They are not arbitrary aliases that can be reordered without changing the meaning of an ID.
docs/color-policy.md:36:Green has two current roles. `--green` supplies matched-card borders and check marks. The Green entry in `FACE_COLORS` supplies faces whose marker points right. Both currently have the value `#8BEA7D`, but they are declared separately in CSS and TypeScript.
docs/color-policy.md:38:A green face therefore does not imply a matched card. It can be a newly revealed, unmatched identity just like a cyan or violet face. The check mark and border are state presentation; the shape, marker and fill are pair identity. This separation is easy to miss when evaluating a screenshot containing only green artwork.
docs/color-policy.md:40:The same issue appears in automated selectors. The current tests find matched cards by `.card.matched` and inspect their border and mark. They do not locate matches by scanning for green pixels. Pixel measurements are useful for contrast and geometry, but game meaning already has an explicit source in props and class names.
docs/color-policy.md:44:The neutral card border becomes the main text color on hover. V16 limits hover to that border change. The matched rule appears after the hover rule and has equal specificity, so the green matched border remains in place while hovered. The artwork itself does not brighten or become dimmer.
docs/color-policy.md:48:The current `.control` class supplies the same text, panel and neutral-border colors to buttons, selects, inputs and the textarea. Their internal browser affordances still depend on the native control renderer. `color-scheme: dark` is declared at the root; it is not a separately implemented light theme. Native controls guide covers that boundary.
docs/color-policy.md:52:`tests/style.e2e.test.ts` compares computed RGB values for the page, panel, card back, face, text, secondary text, border and focus outline. It also checks that hovering changes only border colors and that a matched card stays fully opaque. These checks make a useful distinction between a token being declared and actually reaching its intended element.
docs/color-policy.md:62:A color edit can affect more than the element a reviewer first notices. Changing `--face` alters the revealed background and the marker surround. Changing `--green` affects state marks but leaves the artwork array alone. Changing the artwork array affects SVG appearance and names if its corresponding name table also changes. These are separate edits with separate evidence.
docs/color-policy.md:64:The least ambiguous review records the role, its source declaration and the visible state used to inspect it. A report such as “the top-right marker merges with the square at the small size” is actionable because it identifies geometry and size. “The purple is wrong” leaves open whether the issue is the palette, a face name, a filter, an active transition or a display setting.

import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { isJsonObject } from '../src/game/saved-format.ts';
import { SNAPSHOT_PATH } from './game-fixture.ts';

export async function uiSnapshot() {
  const value: unknown = JSON.parse(await readFile(SNAPSHOT_PATH, 'utf8'));
  if (
    !isJsonObject(value) ||
    typeof value.bestNone !== 'string' ||
    typeof value.winBanner !== 'string' ||
    typeof value.mismatch !== 'string' ||
    typeof value.winningResult !== 'string'
  ) {
    throw new Error('Expected the reviewed UI snapshot.');
  }
  return {
    bestNone: value.bestNone,
    winBanner: value.winBanner,
    mismatch: value.mismatch,
    winningResult: value.winningResult,
  };
}
